    "items": [
      {
        "id": "def-c1-germ-of-a-local-diffeomorphism-at-a-point",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "C\u00b9 germs of local diffeomorphisms at a point",
        "deps": [
          "def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse",
          "def-group",
          "def-subgroup"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $T$ be a one-dimensional manifold equipped with a $C^1$ atlas: its charts\nare homeomorphisms onto open subsets of $\\mathbb R$ whose transition maps are\n$C^1$ with $C^1$ inverses, and \"of class $C^1$\" for maps of $T$ means of class\n$C^1$ in these charts, which is exactly the Euclidean notion of\n[[def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse]]. Fix $x\\in T$.\n\nA **$C^1$ local diffeomorphism of $T$ at $x$ fixing $x$** is a map\n$f:U\\to T$ defined on an open neighbourhood $U\\subseteq T$ of $x$ such that\n$f(U)$ is open, $f:U\\to f(U)$ is a bijection, $f(x)=x$, and both $f$ and\n$f^{-1}:f(U)\\to U$ are of class $C^1$. Two such local diffeomorphisms\n$f:U\\to T$ and $g:V\\to T$ define the same **$C^1$ germ at $x$** when they agree\non some neighbourhood of $x$ contained in $U\\cap V$; write $f\\sim_x g$ for this\nrelation.\n\nThe equivalence classes of $\\sim_x$ are the **$C^1$ germs of local\ndiffeomorphisms of $T$ at $x$**, and their set is denoted\n$\\operatorname{Diff}^1_x(T)$. Composition of representatives induces a binary\noperation\n$$\\operatorname{Diff}^1_x(T)\\times\\operatorname{Diff}^1_x(T)\\to\\operatorname{Diff}^1_x(T),$$\nthe class of $f\\circ g$ being independent of the chosen representatives, and\nwith this operation $\\operatorname{Diff}^1_x(T)$ is a group ([[def-group]])\nwhose identity is the germ of $\\mathrm{id}_T$. Well-definedness of the\noperation, associativity, the two-sided identity and two-sided inverses are\nverified in [[lem-c1-germs-of-local-diffeomorphisms-form-a-group]].\n\nFinally suppose an orientation of a neighbourhood of $x$ is fixed, represented\nby a chart $t$ at $x$ with $t(x)=0$; write $\\tilde f$ for the coordinate\nexpression of a representative $f$. The sign of the derivative\n$\\tilde f'(0)$ is independent of the positively oriented chart and of the\nrepresentative of the germ, so the germs whose representatives have\n$\\tilde f'(0)>0$ in such a chart are well defined. They form a subgroup\n$$\\operatorname{Diff}^{1,+}_x(T)\\le\\operatorname{Diff}^1_x(T)$$\n([[def-subgroup]]), the **orientation-preserving $C^1$ germs at $x$**; both the\ninvariance of the sign and the subgroup property are proved in\n[[lem-c1-germs-of-local-diffeomorphisms-form-a-group]].\n\nOnly the case $\\dim T=1$ is used in this pair, and there only for the transverse\ncoordinate of a codimension-one foliation, where the sign of the derivative is\nthe transverse orientation datum.",
        "strategy": "The germ equivalence, composition, inverse and derivative-sign invariance are verified in [[lem-c1-germs-of-local-diffeomorphisms-form-a-group]]. The $C^1$ local-diffeomorphism and group/subgroup terminology use [[def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse]], [[def-group]] and [[def-subgroup]].",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Danny Calegari, Foliations and the Geometry of 3-Manifolds (Oxford Mathematical Monographs; complete author-hosted PDF)",
              "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/books/foliations/oupbook.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a72.16.2, Theorem 2.119, printed pp. 106\u2013107 (the C\u00b9 interval-germ group used in the local proof)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "justified_by": [
          "lem-c1-germs-of-local-diffeomorphisms-form-a-group"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "def-c1-regular-codimension-one-foliation-and-transverse-orientation",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "C\u00b9 codimension-one regular foliations and transverse orientation",
        "deps": [
          "def-smooth-manifold",
          "def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $M$ be a smooth $n$-manifold ([[def-smooth-manifold]]). A **$C^1$\ncodimension-one foliation atlas** on $M$ is a family of charts\n$$\\varphi_\\alpha=(x_\\alpha,t_\\alpha):U_\\alpha\\to\\mathbb R^{n-1}\\times\\mathbb R,$$\nwith the $U_\\alpha\\subseteq M$ open covering $M$, each $\\varphi_\\alpha$ a\nhomeomorphism onto its image whose inverse is of class $C^1$ and whose\ncomponents are of class $C^1$ ([[def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse]]), such that\nfor every $\\alpha,\\beta$ with $U_\\alpha\\cap U_\\beta\\ne\\varnothing$ the transition\nmap, wherever defined, has the form\n$$(x_\\beta,t_\\beta)=\\bigl(g_{\\beta\\alpha}(x_\\alpha,t_\\alpha),\\,h_{\\beta\\alpha}(t_\\alpha)\\bigr),$$\nwhere $g_{\\beta\\alpha}$ is of class $C^1$ and $h_{\\beta\\alpha}$ is a\none-dimensional $C^1$ local diffeomorphism of intervals\n([[def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse]]). Thus the second coordinate of a chart\ndepends only on the old second coordinate, and charts change along the first\ncoordinates arbitrarily inside the slices of constant second coordinate.\n\nCharts of such an atlas are **foliation charts**. For a foliation chart\n$\\varphi_\\alpha$ and $c\\in\\mathbb R$ the set of points of $U_\\alpha$ whose\nsecond coordinate equals $c$ is a **slice**, and each of its connected\ncomponents is a **plaque** of the atlas. Two points of $M$ are said to be\n**plaque-chain equivalent** when they can be joined by a finite chain\n$P_0,\\dots,P_k$ of plaques with $P_{i-1}\\cap P_i\\ne\\varnothing$ for\n$1\\le i\\le k$; this is an equivalence relation. Its equivalence classes are the\n**leaves** of the atlas, and the leaf through a point $p$ is written $L_p$. A\n**$C^1$ codimension-one foliation** $F$ of $M$ is the leaf decomposition\ndetermined by one such atlas; by\n[[lem-c1-foliated-atlas-preserves-plaque-equivalence-and-transverse-orientation]]\nthe plaques and the leaf decomposition are unchanged if the atlas is replaced\nby any other $C^1$ foliation atlas on $M$ with the same slices, so that plaques\nand leaves are attributes of $F$ and not of a chosen atlas.\n\nThe foliation $F$ is **transversely oriented**, or **co-oriented**, when the\ntransverse coordinates can be signed consistently. Concretely, $F$ is\ntransversely oriented when there is a foliation atlas for $M$ presenting $F$ in\nwhich every transition germ $h_{\\beta\\alpha}$ is orientation-preserving:\n$h_{\\beta\\alpha}'(t)>0$ at every $t$ in its domain, equivalently each\n$h_{\\beta\\alpha}$ is increasing. The signed form of the condition allows one to\nchoose, for every chart of a foliation atlas, a locally constant sign\n$\\varepsilon_\\alpha\\in\\{+,-\\}$ and to replace the transverse coordinate\n$t_\\alpha$ by $\\varepsilon_\\alpha t_\\alpha$; all transitions become increasing\nprecisely when, on every nonempty overlap, the two sign choices compensate the\nsign of $h_{\\beta\\alpha}'$. The equivalence of these formulations, and the fact\nthat transverse orientability is likewise independent of the atlas, are proved in\n[[lem-c1-foliated-atlas-preserves-plaque-equivalence-and-transverse-orientation]].\n\nIn a transversely oriented foliation the two sides of each plaque carry a\nconsistent sign, and the local transversals to the plaques are ordered in a\nway respected by all plaque transports; this is the only use made of transverse\norientation in this pair. All foliations below are $C^1$ and regular in the\nabove sense.",
        "strategy": "The chart changes and plaque-chain relation are verified in [[lem-c1-foliated-atlas-preserves-plaque-equivalence-and-transverse-orientation]]. The $C^1$-map and local-inverse terms use [[def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse]], and the ambient chart convention uses [[def-smooth-manifold]]. This is a C\u00b9 regularity specialization of the foliation-atlas definition; transverse orientation is exactly consistent orientation of the one-dimensional normal coordinate.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Gabai, Commentary on Thurston's Foliations and the Thurston norm",
              "url": "https://web.math.princeton.edu/facultypapers/Gabai/Commentary-Thurston-Foliations.pdf",
              "locator": "Theorem 0.8, PDF p. 2 (C\u00b9 codimension-one foliation hypotheses); the chart formulation is stated here locally"
            },
            {
              "title": "Danny Calegari, Foliations and the Geometry of 3-Manifolds (Oxford Mathematical Monographs; complete author-hosted PDF)",
              "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/books/foliations/oupbook.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a74.2, printed pp. 140\u2013143 (foliation charts, plaques and transverse holonomy)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "justified_by": [
          "lem-c1-foliated-atlas-preserves-plaque-equivalence-and-transverse-orientation"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "The countable-choice principle used in the foliation pair",
        "deps": [
          "def-choice-function",
          "def-function",
          "def-countable-choice"
        ],
        "statement": "The **countable choice principle** used throughout this pair, written\n$\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the assertion:\n\n> For every sequence $(A_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb N}$ of nonempty sets there is a\n> function $c$ with domain $\\mathbb N$ such that $c(n)\\in A_n$ for every\n> $n\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nHere \"sequence\" means a function on $\\mathbb N$ ([[def-function]]) and \"choice\nfunction\" is the notion of [[def-choice-function]]; thus the displayed\nstatement says that every sequence of nonempty sets has a choice function.\n\nThis is the sequence formulation of countable choice. The equivalent\nnonempty-product formulation, that $\\prod_{n\\in\\mathbb N}A_n$ is nonempty for\nevery sequence $(A_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb N}$ of nonempty sets, is verified in\n[[lem-countable-choice-sequence-and-product-formulations-are-equivalent]]; that\nlemma is recorded as the well-definedness certificate of the present\ndefinition.\n\nIn this pair $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is a stated hypothesis of the foliation\ntheorems and of the items whose proof selects countably many plaque data. It is\nnot assumed where a proof does not use it, and the items that consume it state\nthe hypothesis explicitly.\n\nThis pair-local carrier is retained deliberately: it restates the published\n[[def-countable-choice]] in exactly the sequence form consumed by the\nfoliation items, and the published definition supplies the same principle. The\nretention and its cross-batch consequences are recorded in the Step-3 report\nof this pair (finding F4).",
        "strategy": "This is the sequence formulation of countable choice: a choice function on the index set selects one element from each member of the sequence. The terms function and choice function use [[def-function]] and [[def-choice-function]]. The product formulation is proved equivalent in [[lem-countable-choice-sequence-and-product-formulations-are-equivalent]].",
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "D. H. Fremlin, Measure Theory, Chapter 56",
              "url": "https://www1.essex.ac.uk/maths/people/fremlin/chap56.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Axiom of countable choice (Wikipedia)",
              "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axiom_of_countable_choice"
            }
          ]
        },
        "justified_by": [
          "lem-countable-choice-sequence-and-product-formulations-are-equivalent"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "def-saturated-neighbourhood-of-a-leaf",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Saturated neighbourhoods of a leaf",
        "deps": [
          "def-regular-foliation-atlas",
          "def-leaf-of-a-regular-foliation"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $F$ be a regular foliation of a smooth manifold $M$\n([[def-regular-foliation-atlas]]) and let $L$ be a leaf of $F$\n([[def-leaf-of-a-regular-foliation]]).\n\nAn open set $U\\subseteq M$ is **saturated**, or **invariant**, for $F$ when it\nis a union of leaves of $F$; equivalently, when for every $x\\in U$ the whole\nleaf of $F$ through $x$ is contained in $U$. The equivalence is immediate from\nthe definitions: a union of leaves has the pointwise property, and conversely\nthe set of leaves meeting $U$ covers $U$ by the pointwise property, so $U$ is\ntheir union.\n\nA **saturated neighbourhood of $L$** is a saturated open set $U\\subseteq M$\nwith $L\\subseteq U$. If $U$ is saturated then $F$ restricts to a regular\nfoliation of $U$: the plaques of $F$ that meet $U$ form a foliation atlas of the\nopen submanifold $U$ ([[def-regular-foliation-atlas]]), and the leaves of the\nrestriction are the connected components of the plaque partition of $U$, which\nare exactly the leaves of $F$ that meet $U$ ([[def-leaf-of-a-regular-foliation]]).\n\nThe neighbourhood conclusion of Reeb stability below is stated as the\nexistence, for every neighbourhood $W$ of $L$ in $M$, of a saturated\nneighbourhood $U\\subseteq W$ of $L$; this property of $L$ is recorded\nseparately below as **stability** of the leaf.",
        "strategy": "Unfold the published leaf vocabulary: a leaf is a maximal connected integral manifold, saturated just says $U$ is a union of such leaves, and the equivalence with the pointwise condition is immediate from maximality. Saturation makes $U\\cup$-closed and open, and the restriction statement follows since the leaves of $F$ meeting $U$ are the connected components of the plaque partition of $U$. No choice principle is used.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Leiden NCG seminar, Noncommutative Geometry of Foliations (2023 seminar notes; complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://ncg-leiden.github.io/foliation2023/foliation_notes.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a71.3.6, printed p. 10 (PDF p. 11); \u00a72.1, printed pp. 11\u201314 (PDF pp. 12\u201315); \u00a72.2, printed pp. 14\u201315 (PDF pp. 15\u201316)"
            },
            {
              "title": "Danny Calegari, Foliations and the Geometry of 3-Manifolds (Oxford Mathematical Monographs; complete author-hosted PDF)",
              "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/books/foliations/oupbook.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a74.2, printed pp. 140\u2013143 (PDF pp. 149\u2013152); \u00a74.3, printed pp. 144\u2013145 (PDF pp. 153\u2013154), Example 4.7; Lemma 4.24, printed p. 155 (PDF p. 164)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-images-of-finitely-generated-and-finite-groups-are-finitely-generated-and-finite",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Images of finitely generated and of finite groups are finitely generated and finite",
        "status": "draft",
        "origin": "pipeline",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-generated"
        },
        "deps": [
          "def-group-homomorphism",
          "lem-group-homomorphism-basic-properties",
          "def-generated-subgroup",
          "def-subgroup",
          "def-finitely-generated-group",
          "def-kernel-and-image-of-group-homomorphism",
          "thm-first-isomorphism-theorem-groups",
          "thm-image-subgroup-and-kernel-normal",
          "cor-order-of-a-quotient-group",
          "def-finite-cardinality",
          "cor-cardinality-of-the-power-set",
          "thm-subset-of-a-finite-set",
          "def-injection-surjection-bijection"
        ],
        "justified_by": [],
        "aliases": [],
        "landmark": false,
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Finitely generated group (Wikipedia)",
              "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finitely_generated_group"
            },
            {
              "title": "T. W. Judson, Abstract Algebra: Theory and Applications, Factor Groups and Normal Subgroups (LibreTexts)",
              "url": "https://math.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Abstract_and_Geometric_Algebra/Abstract_Algebra%253A_Theory_and_Applications_%28Judson%29/10%253A_Normal_Subgroups_and_Factor_Groups/10.01%253A_Factor_Groups_and_Normal_Subgroups"
            }
          ]
        },
        "statement": "Let $\\varphi:G\\to H$ be a group homomorphism\n([[def-group-homomorphism]]). Then:\n\n1. if $G$ is finitely generated ([[def-finitely-generated-group]]), then its\n   image $\\operatorname{im}\\varphi\\le H$\n   ([[def-kernel-and-image-of-group-homomorphism]]) is finitely generated;\n2. if $G$ is finite ([[def-finite-cardinality]]), then\n   $\\operatorname{im}\\varphi$ is finite.",
        "strategy": "Finite word expansion proves the generated-subgroup image identity. Finite image sets are finite by injection into the finite power set of the domain; finite group images are quotient groups. No choice principle is required.",
        "dependency_level": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "def-foliation-tangent-to-the-boundary-of-a-manifold-with-boundary",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Smooth foliations tangent to the boundary",
        "deps": [
          "def-regular-foliation-atlas",
          "def-smooth-chart-atlas-and-structure-on-a-manifold-with-boundary",
          "def-topological-manifold-with-boundary",
          "thm-the-boundary-is-a-closed-embedded-smooth-n-minus-one-manifold",
          "def-smooth-distribution-on-a-manifold",
          "def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]]). Let $W$ be a smooth\n$n$-manifold with boundary, with boundary charts modelled on the half-space\n$\\mathbb H^n=\\mathbb R^{n-1}\\times[0,\\infty)$ and smooth structure as in\n[[def-smooth-chart-atlas-and-structure-on-a-manifold-with-boundary]] and\n[[def-topological-manifold-with-boundary]]; thus\n$\\partial W$ is a closed embedded smooth $(n-1)$-manifold\n([[thm-the-boundary-is-a-closed-embedded-smooth-n-minus-one-manifold]]).\n\nFix $1\\le q\\le n$ and split $\\mathbb H^n=\\mathbb R^{n-q}\\times\\mathbb H^q$. A\n**regular foliation of $W$ tangent to $\\partial W$ of codimension $q$** is a\nregular foliation atlas for the smooth structure of $W$\n([[def-regular-foliation-atlas]]) whose charts are relatively open subsets of\n$\\mathbb H^n$ and whose transition maps are compatible with the standard\ndecomposition of $\\mathbb H^n$ into the model plaques\n$\\mathbb R^{n-q}\\times\\{y\\}$, $y\\in\\mathbb H^q$; that is, on every overlap the\ncoordinates of the second factor depend only on the second coordinates of the\nfirst factor, exactly as for a foliation atlas on a boundaryless manifold, and\nthe plaque decomposition restricts to the half-space.\n\nThe model plaques meet $\\partial\\mathbb H^n=\\mathbb R^{n-q}\\times\\partial\n\\mathbb H^q$ in the sets $\\mathbb R^{n-q}\\times\\{y\\}$ with\n$y\\in\\partial\\mathbb H^q$. Consequently, near a boundary point of $W$ the\nleaves of $F$ are the intersections of the model plaques with the half-space;\nthe boundary $\\partial W$ is a union of leaves of $F$, and each such\nboundary leaf, being a plaque intersection with a hyperplane, inherits the\nstructure of a regular foliation of codimension $q-1$; the tangent distribution\n$TF$ of the foliation ([[def-smooth-distribution-on-a-manifold]]) is tangent to\nthe boundary along $\\partial W$ in the sense that $T_p\\partial W$ contains\n$D_p=TF_p$ for $p\\in\\partial W$, while $D_p$ is transverse to the normal\ndirection of the boundary. A leaf of the foliation restricted to the interior\n$\\operatorname{Int}W$ is a leaf of $F$, and it need not meet $\\partial W$.\n\nThe pair uses this vocabulary only for $q=1$: the Reeb foliations of the solid\ntorus and its gluing are tangent to the boundary, and the boundary torus of a\nReeb component is a single leaf.",
        "strategy": "Transfer the published atlas definition to the half-space model: a chart for $W$ is a relatively open subset of $\\mathbb H^n$ together with a diffeomorphism onto its image, and the model decomposition of $\\mathbb H^n$ restricts to a plaque decomposition on the interior and on the boundary hyperplane $\\mathbb R^{n-q}\\times\\partial\\mathbb H^q$, so the boundary is a union of leaves and carries its own regular foliation of leaf dimension $n-q$ and boundary codimension $(n-1)-(n-q)=q-1$. Consistency of the definitions is a local computation in half-space charts; the collar and boundary-submanifold items of the manifolds-with-boundary page supply the identification of $\\partial W$ with a smooth manifold. No additional choice principle is used.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Leiden NCG seminar, Noncommutative Geometry of Foliations (2023 seminar notes; complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://ncg-leiden.github.io/foliation2023/foliation_notes.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a71.3.6, printed p. 10 (PDF p. 11); \u00a72.1, printed pp. 11\u201314 (PDF pp. 12\u201315); \u00a72.2, printed pp. 14\u201315 (PDF pp. 15\u201316)"
            },
            {
              "title": "Tomasz Mrowka, MIT 18.965 Differential Topology, lecture notes (complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://math.mit.edu/~mrowka/math965lectnote.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a7\u00a720\u201323, PDF pp. 52\u201356"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "def-stable-leaf-of-a-foliation",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Stable leaves",
        "deps": [
          "def-saturated-neighbourhood-of-a-leaf",
          "def-regular-foliation-atlas"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $F$ be a regular foliation of a smooth manifold $M$\n([[def-regular-foliation-atlas]]) and let $L$ be a leaf of $F$.\n\nThe leaf $L$ is **stable** when every open neighbourhood $W$ of $L$ in $M$\ncontains a saturated neighbourhood of $L$\n([[def-saturated-neighbourhood-of-a-leaf]]); equivalently, when the saturated\nneighbourhoods of $L$ form a fundamental system of neighbourhoods of $L$.\n\nMore generally, a subset $B\\subseteq M$ is **stable in the sense of Reeb** when\nfor every open neighbourhood $W$ of $B$ there is an open neighbourhood\n$W'\\subseteq W$ of $B$ such that every leaf of $F$ meeting $W'$ is contained in\n$W$. For a leaf $B=L$ this is equivalent to stability of $L$: if such a $W'$\nexists, then its saturation\n$U=\\bigcup\\{L' : L'\\text{ a leaf and } L'\\cap W'\\neq\\varnothing\\}$ is open and saturated (each box carries an open set to its open plaque saturation, and finite plaque transport carries this property along every leaf), contains $L$, and satisfies $U\\subseteq W$ by the property of $W'$,\nso $U$ is a saturated neighbourhood of $L$ inside $W$; conversely a saturated\nneighbourhood $U\\subseteq W$ of $L$ is itself a neighbourhood $W'\\subseteq W$ of\n$L$ every leaf meeting which is contained in $U\\subseteq W$.\n\nStability of a leaf is a neighbourhood property: it depends only on the germ of\nthe foliation along $L$, since both quantifiers involve neighbourhoods of $L$.\nThis is the property that the local and global Reeb stability theorems below\nestablish under their finiteness hypotheses.",
        "strategy": "The equivalence is a two-line saturation argument: the saturation $\\bigcup\\{L' : L'\\cap W'\\neq\\varnothing\\}$ of the Reeb neighbourhood $W'$ is contained in $W$ by hypothesis and is saturated and open, so it is a saturated neighbourhood of $L$; conversely a saturated neighbourhood $U\\subseteq W$ is itself a Reeb neighbourhood. The definition records exactly the conclusion used by the global stability theorem. No choice principle is used.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Leiden NCG seminar, Noncommutative Geometry of Foliations (2023 seminar notes; complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://ncg-leiden.github.io/foliation2023/foliation_notes.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a71.3.6, printed p. 10 (PDF p. 11); \u00a72.1, printed pp. 11\u201314 (PDF pp. 12\u201315); \u00a72.2, printed pp. 14\u201315 (PDF pp. 15\u201316)"
            },
            {
              "title": "Danny Calegari, Foliations and the Geometry of 3-Manifolds (Oxford Mathematical Monographs; complete author-hosted PDF)",
              "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/books/foliations/oupbook.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a74.2, printed pp. 140\u2013143 (PDF pp. 149\u2013152); \u00a74.3, printed pp. 144\u2013145 (PDF pp. 153\u2013154), Example 4.7; Lemma 4.24, printed p. 155 (PDF p. 164)"
            }
          ]
        },
