      {
        "id": "lem-a-closed-smooth-manifold-has-the-homotopy-type-of-a-finite-cw-complex",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "A closed smooth manifold has the homotopy type of a finite CW complex",
        "deps": [
          "cor-every-compact-smooth-manifold-admits-an-excellent-morse-function",
          "cor-a-morse-function-on-a-compact-manifold-has-finitely-many-critical-points",
          "cor-one-critical-point-cell-attachment-homotopy-type",
          "def-morse-function-and-excellent-morse-function",
          "def-critical-point-and-critical-value-of-a-smooth-function",
          "def-cell-attachment-by-a-characteristic-map",
          "def-cw-complex-with-closure-finiteness-and-weak-topology",
          "def-compact-space",
          "def-smooth-manifold",
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "lem-axiom-of-choice-implies-countable-choice"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $M$ be a closed smooth\nmanifold ([[def-smooth-manifold]]). Then $M$ has the homotopy type of a finite\nCW complex ([[def-cw-complex-with-closure-finiteness-and-weak-topology]]).",
        "strategy": "Let $c_1<\\dots<c_k$ be the critical values of $f$; they are distinct because $f$ is excellent ([[def-morse-function-and-excellent-morse-function]]) and finitely many because $M$ is compact ([[cor-a-morse-function-on-a-compact-manifold-has-finitely-many-critical-points]]). Choose regular values $a_0<c_1<a_1<c_2<\\dots<c_k<a_k$ with $a_0<\\min f$ and $a_k>\\max f$; this is possible because only finitely many values are critical, and then $M^{a_0}=\\varnothing$ and $M^{a_k}=M$. For each $j$ the compact band $f^{-1}([a_{j-1},a_j])$ has regular boundary values and contains exactly one critical point $p_j$, nondegenerate by the Morse condition, so the one-critical-point compact-band form of the handle-attachment theorem ([[cor-one-critical-point-cell-attachment-homotopy-type]]) gives a homotopy equivalence $M^{a_j}\\simeq M^{a_{j-1}}\\cup e^{\\lambda_j}$ with one cell attached along the transported attaching sphere, compatible with the lower sublevel up to homotopy of pairs. Inducting on $j$ and using [[def-cell-attachment-by-a-characteristic-map]] and the closure-finiteness and weak topology of [[def-cw-complex-with-closure-finiteness-and-weak-topology]], each sublevel $M^{a_j}$ has the homotopy type of the finite CW complex $X_j=X_{j-1}\\cup e^{\\lambda_j}$; since $M=M^{a_k}$, the manifold $M$ has the homotopy type of the finite CW complex $X_k$ with one $\\lambda_j$-cell for each $j$. Finiteness of the cell set is finiteness of the critical set. The axiom of choice is consumed through [[cor-every-compact-smooth-manifold-admits-an-excellent-morse-function]], and the attachments use the countable-choice hypothesis of the published handle-attachment items, which follows from full AC by [[lem-axiom-of-choice-implies-countable-choice]].",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "literature-derived"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "John Milnor, Morse Theory (Annals of Mathematics Studies 51; complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://www.maths.ed.ac.uk/~v1ranick/papers/milnmors.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a73, Theorem 3.5, printed pp. 20\u201321 (CW homotopy type with one cell per critical point); \u00a76, printed pp. 28\u201335 (existence of Morse functions on compact manifolds)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-a-non-closed-leaf-of-a-codimension-one-foliation-meets-a-closed-transversal",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "A non-closed leaf of a codimension-one foliation meets a closed transversal",
        "deps": [
          "def-regular-foliation-atlas",
          "def-leaf-of-a-regular-foliation",
          "prop-embedded-leaves-need-not-be-closed-and-leaves-need-not-be-embedded",
          "def-smooth-embedding",
          "def-embedded-submanifold-and-slice-chart",
          "def-compact-space",
          "def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair",
          "def-transversely-oriented-codimension-one-foliation"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]]). Let $F$ be a smooth cooriented codimension-one foliation of a smooth manifold $M$, and let $A$ be a leaf that is not a closed subset of $M$. There is a smooth embedded circle transverse to $F$ that meets $A$.\n\nThe circle need not lie in every prescribed open neighborhood of $A$; the localization claim is false. The stronger finite-compact-barrier version needed in compact ambient manifolds is constructed directly in the global closedness lemma.",
        "strategy": "Follow the proof of Calegari, Lemma 4.24 (printed p. 155): a non-closed leaf accumulates somewhere, so there is a product chart $U$ meeting $\\lambda$ in at least two plaques through which one passes from one plaque of $\\lambda$ to another along a short transversal segment $\\tau$; let $\\gamma_0$ be an embedded arc in $\\lambda$ joining the two endpoints of $\\tau$ and contained in a neighbourhood on which the product structure is defined. Replacing one of the two segments of $\\tau$ (the one on the negative side of $\\gamma_0$) by the graph $\\tau'(t)=(\\gamma_0(t),t)$ inside a rectangle foliated by transversals gives an embedded closed curve $\\tau'\\cup(\\tau\\smallsetminus\\tau^-)$ that is transverse to $F$ and meets $\\lambda$; the isotopy from $\\tau^-$ to $\\tau'$ can be performed in an arbitrarily small neighbourhood of $\\gamma_0$ and of $U$, so $\\gamma$ lies in an arbitrarily small neighbourhood of $\\lambda$. Only the standing $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is used.\n\nHere $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ denotes the countable-choice principle [[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]].",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Danny Calegari, Foliations and the Geometry of 3-Manifolds (Oxford Mathematical Monographs; complete author-hosted PDF)",
              "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/books/foliations/oupbook.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a74.3, Example 4.23 and Lemma 4.24 with its complete proof, printed pp. 154\u2013155 (PDF pp. 163\u2013164)"
            },
            {
              "title": "Tomasz Mrowka, MIT 18.965 Differential Topology, lecture notes (complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://math.mit.edu/~mrowka/math965lectnote.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a7\u00a720\u201323, PDF pp. 52\u201356 (closed transversals through recurrent leaves)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-c1-holonomy-is-a-well-defined-representation-into-transverse-germs",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Holonomy of a C\u00b9 foliation is a representation into C\u00b9 transverse germs",
        "deps": [
          "def-c1-regular-codimension-one-foliation-and-transverse-orientation",
          "def-c1-germ-of-a-local-diffeomorphism-at-a-point",
          "lem-c1-germs-of-local-diffeomorphisms-form-a-group",
          "def-based-loops-and-fundamental-group"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $F$ be a transversely oriented $C^1$ codimension-one foliation, $L$ a\nleaf, $x\\in L$, and $T$ a local $C^1$ transversal to $F$ at $x$. Plaque\ntransport along leafwise loops defines a homomorphism\n$$\\rho_x:\\pi_1(L,x)\\to\\operatorname{Diff}^{1,+}_x(T)$$\nthat is independent of the chosen chains of foliation charts and invariant\nunder leafwise homotopies relative to endpoints.",
        "strategy": "A finite chain of C\u00b9 foliation charts transports a sufficiently small transversal by a finite composition of C\u00b9 transverse coordinate changes, hence gives a C\u00b9 local diffeomorphism germ; coorientation makes every factor orientation-preserving ([[def-c1-regular-codimension-one-foliation-and-transverse-orientation]], [[def-c1-germ-of-a-local-diffeomorphism-at-a-point]], [[lem-c1-germs-of-local-diffeomorphisms-form-a-group]]). Refining a chart chain leaves the composite germ unchanged. For a leafwise homotopy of paths, compactness of the parameter square gives a finite subdivision into rectangles each lying in one foliation chart; transport around each rectangle is the identity because the transverse coordinate is constant along plaques. Cancelling internal edges proves homotopy invariance. Concatenating loops concatenates the transverse maps, so $\\rho_x$ is a homomorphism, with composition order fixed by the path-concatenation convention of [[def-based-loops-and-fundamental-group]].",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Danny Calegari, Foliations and the Geometry of 3-Manifolds (Oxford Mathematical Monographs; complete author-hosted PDF)",
              "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/books/foliations/oupbook.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a74.2, printed pp. 140\u2013143 (construction and homotopy invariance of holonomy transport)"
            },
            {
              "title": "David Gabai, Commentary on Thurston's Foliations and the Thurston norm",
              "url": "https://web.math.princeton.edu/facultypapers/Gabai/Commentary-Thurston-Foliations.pdf",
              "locator": "Theorem 0.8, PDF p. 2 (C\u00b9 foliation setting)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-germs-of-orientation-preserving-diffeomorphisms-of-the-line-at-zero-are-torsion-free",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Germs of orientation-preserving diffeomorphisms of the line at zero are torsion-free",
        "deps": [
          "def-germ-of-a-local-diffeomorphism-at-a-point",
          "lem-germs-of-local-diffeomorphisms-form-a-group",
          "def-diffeomorphism-and-local-diffeomorphism-of-manifolds"
        ],
        "statement": "Every finite subgroup of the group $\\operatorname{Diff}^+_0(\\mathbb R,0)$ of\ngerms at $0$ of orientation-preserving local diffeomorphisms of $\\mathbb R$\nfixing $0$ is trivial: if $h$ is such a germ and $h^n=\\mathrm{id}$ in the group\nof germs for some $n\\ge1$, then $h=\\mathrm{id}$. Equivalently, the group of\ngerms of orientation-preserving local diffeomorphisms fixing a point of a\none-dimensional manifold is torsion-free.",
        "strategy": "Fix a positive integer $n$ and suppose $h^n$ is the identity germ. Choose an increasing representative $h$ near $0$; shrink a neighborhood so all its first $n$ iterates are defined there and $h^n(t)=t$. If $h$ were not the identity germ, there would be arbitrarily small $t$ on one of the two sides of $0$ with $h(t)\\ne t$. If $h(t)>t$, monotonicity gives $h^{k+1}(t)>h^k(t)$ for every $k<n$; if $h(t)<t$, the inequalities are reversed. Either case contradicts $h^n(t)=t$. Thus every finite-order germ is identity, and each element of a finite subgroup has finite order, so the subgroup is trivial. The argument requires only finite restrictions and no choice principle.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "literature-derived"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Leiden NCG seminar, Noncommutative Geometry of Foliations (2023 seminar notes; complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://ncg-leiden.github.io/foliation2023/foliation_notes.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a72.1\u2013\u00a72.2, printed pp. 11\u201315 (one-dimensional germs and their finite subgroups)"
            },
            {
              "title": "Danny Calegari, Foliations and the Geometry of 3-Manifolds (Oxford Mathematical Monographs; complete author-hosted PDF)",
              "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/books/foliations/oupbook.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a74.2, printed pp. 140\u2013143 (holonomy germs of one-dimensional transversals)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-oriented-intersection-detects-nonvanishing-rational-homology",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "A co-oriented closed transversal detects nonvanishing rational homology of a compact leaf",
        "deps": [
          "def-oriented-intersection-number",
          "thm-oriented-intersection-number-is-homotopy-invariant",
          "cor-a-null-cobordant-cycle-has-zero-intersection-with-a-disjoint-boundary",
          "lem-compact-transverse-complementary-intersections-are-finite",
          "def-transversely-oriented-codimension-one-foliation",
          "def-smooth-embedding",
          "def-singular-chain-complex-and-singular-homology",
          "def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair",
          "def-oriented-smooth-manifold-and-oriented-chart",
          "lem-relative-smoothing-of-a-continuous-simplex-along-its-faces",
          "lem-compact-c1-foliation-leaf-is-an-embedded-hypersurface"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]]). Let $F$ be a\ntransversely oriented smooth codimension-one foliation of a closed oriented\n$n$-manifold $M$, $n\\ge2$. Orient a closed immersed transversal $\\gamma$ and\norient every compact hypersurface leaf $S$ by the ambient orientation together\nwith the positive transverse normal. Then:\n\n1. the intersection count $I(\\gamma,S)$ has one sign and is nonzero whenever\n   $\\gamma$ meets $S$;\n2. $I(\\gamma,S)$ depends only on the rational homology classes\n   $[\\gamma]\\in H_1(M;\\mathbb Q)$ and $[S]\\in H_{n-1}(M;\\mathbb Q)$;\n3. if $\\gamma$ misses compact leaves $S_1,\\dots,S_k$ but meets the compact leaf\n   $S$, then $[S]$ lies outside the rational span of\n   $[S_1],\\dots,[S_k]$ in $H_{n-1}(M;\\mathbb Q)$;\n4. the same conclusions hold for a finite union of compact leaves carrying\n   these consistent orientations.",
        "strategy": "Transversality and compactness make intersections finite; the induced leaf orientation and positive direction of \u03b3 give the same local determinant sign at every point. Homology invariance is a finite-chain argument, not merely homotopy invariance. Smooth any specified finite rational bounding chain compatibly on faces by the declared relative simplex-smoothing supplier; clear denominators first if needed. Use finite smooth perturbations, compatible on common faces, transverse to the fixed smooth immersed \u03b3, preserving its given transverse boundary intersections. Pulling back \u03b3 on each n-simplex gives a compact oriented one-dimensional inverse-image chain. Its boundary points on common simplex faces cancel with the rational coefficients, while the remaining boundary is exactly the intersection chain of \u03b3 with the bounding hypersurface cycle. Every finite oriented interval has total signed boundary zero; subdividing the compact one-dimensional inverse images into finitely many arcs therefore proves I(\u03b3,\u2202C)=0. The same argument applies to changing the closed \u03b3 by a finite bounding chain. No perturbation is claimed to remove prescribed boundary intersections. Bilinearity now proves nonvanishing and the span conclusion by evaluating a hypothetical linear relation against \u03b3. All choices are finite for the specified cycles/chain; the standing AC\u03c9 suffices. Finite immersed transversal self-crossings do not change this argument, which uses its parametrization.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Danny Calegari, Foliations and the Geometry of 3-Manifolds (Oxford Mathematical Monographs; complete author-hosted PDF)",
              "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/books/foliations/oupbook.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a74.2, printed pp. 140\u2013143 (co-oriented intersections and the Novikov limit argument)"
            },
            {
              "title": "John Milnor, Morse Theory (Annals of Mathematics Studies 51; complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://www.maths.ed.ac.uk/~v1ranick/papers/milnmors.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a73, printed pp. 17\u201321 (intersection-theoretic obstructions and finite cell counts)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "prop-mapping-torus-foliations-realize-global-reeb-stable-examples",
        "kind": "proposition",
        "title": "Mapping torus foliations realize global Reeb stable examples",
        "deps": [
          "prop-quotient-foliation-under-a-free-proper-foliated-action",
          "def-suspension-foliation-of-a-group-action",
          "def-diffeomorphism-and-local-diffeomorphism-of-manifolds",
          "def-group-action",
          "prop-products-of-smooth-manifolds-have-a-canonical-product-smooth-structure",
          "def-regular-foliation-atlas",
          "def-euclidean-spheres-and-closed-balls",
          "def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]]). Let $L$ be a nonempty connected closed\nsmooth manifold and $f:L\\to L$ a diffeomorphism\n([[def-diffeomorphism-and-local-diffeomorphism-of-manifolds]]). Let $\\mathbb Z$\nact on $L\\times\\mathbb R$ by $n\\cdot(x,t):=(f^n(x),t+n)$ and let\n$M:=(L\\times\\mathbb R)/\\mathbb Z$ be the associated **mapping torus**. Then:\n\n1. $M$ is a closed smooth manifold;\n2. the product foliation of $L\\times\\mathbb R$ by the slices $L\\times\\{t\\}$ is\n   invariant under the action and descends to a codimension-one regular\n   foliation $F_f$ of $M$ whose leaves are the images of the slices;\n3. every leaf of $F_f$ is compact and diffeomorphic to $L$, with trivial\n   holonomy;\n4. the projection $(x,t)\\mapsto t\\bmod 1$ descends to a smooth map $M\\to S^1$\n   whose fibres are exactly the leaves, exhibiting $F_f$ as the fibre foliation\n   of a locally trivial fibre bundle over $S^1$ with fibre $L$;\n5. the suspension/base direction (the image of $\\partial/\\partial t$) is\n   transverse to the fibres, and its first-return map on the fibre\n   $L\\times\\{0\\}$ is $f^{-1}$, the monodromy for this quotient convention.\n\nThe foliation has a compact leaf with trivial holonomy, and it realizes the circle-fibration conclusion directly. The finite-fundamental-group global theorem applies to this foliation when $L$ is connected with finite fundamental group. Bundles over $S^1$ with fibre $L$ are classified up to isomorphism by\nthe conjugacy class of the monodromy in $\\pi_0\\operatorname{Diff}(L)$; hence\nthis bundle is the trivial bundle $L\\times S^1$ if and only if $f$ is isotopic\nto the identity, and monodromies with nonconjugate classes in $\\pi_0\\operatorname{Diff}(L)$ realize distinct bundle structures over the fixed oriented base circle. No claim is made about $M$ as a bare manifold.",
        "strategy": "The $\\mathbb Z$-action is free and properly discontinuous (the $t$-coordinates of its translates of a product neighbourhood are disjoint), i.e. a covering-space action, and it preserves the product foliation by the slices because $n\\cdot(L\\times\\{t\\})=L\\times\\{t+n\\}$; [[prop-quotient-foliation-under-a-free-proper-foliated-action]] then provides the smooth structure on $M$ and the descended regular foliation. Leaves are the images of the slices $L\\times\\{t\\}$, each diffeomorphic to $L$ and compact. Holonomy is trivial because the leafwise transport in the product $L\\times\\mathbb R$ is the identity and the quotient is taken by deck transformations acting leafwise. The map $(x,t)\\mapsto t\\bmod 1$ is invariant and descends; its fibres are the leaves, and over an interval of the base the product structure $L\\times I$ trivializes it, which is local triviality. The vector field $\\partial/\\partial t$ is invariant and transverse; after one unit of its flow the point $(x,0)$ represents $(f^{-1}(x),0)$, so the first-return map is $f^{-1}$ for the displayed action convention. The classification of $L$-bundles over $S^1$ up to bundle isomorphism by conjugacy classes in $\\pi_0\\operatorname{Diff}(L)$ (equivalently: a bundle isomorphism over the identity of the base identifies the monodromies up to isotopy) gives the stated triviality criterion; only the bundle structure is classified, and no claim about $M$ as a bare manifold is made. Only the standing $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is used.\n\nHere $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ denotes the countable-choice principle [[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]].",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "literature-derived"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Danny Calegari, Foliations and the Geometry of 3-Manifolds (Oxford Mathematical Monographs; complete author-hosted PDF)",
              "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/books/foliations/oupbook.pdf",
              "locator": "the section on holonomy, printed pp. 140\u2013143 (foliated circle bundles and the representation \u2194 bundle correspondence, the representation correspondence)"
            },
            {
              "title": "Leiden NCG seminar, Noncommutative Geometry of Foliations (2023 seminar notes; complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://ncg-leiden.github.io/foliation2023/foliation_notes.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a72.1\u2013\u00a72.2, printed pp. 11\u201315 (suspension foliations and their holonomy)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "prop-reeb-foliation-of-the-solid-torus-has-the-boundary-as-a-leaf",
        "kind": "proposition",
        "title": "The Reeb foliation of the solid torus has the boundary as a leaf",
        "deps": [
          "def-foliation-tangent-to-the-boundary-of-a-manifold-with-boundary",
          "cor-kernel-of-a-constant-rank-submersion-is-integrable",
          "thm-regular-foliations-and-integrable-distributions-correspond",
          "prop-quotient-foliation-under-a-free-proper-foliated-action",
          "def-euclidean-spheres-and-closed-balls",
          "def-two-dimensional-torus",
          "def-embedded-submanifold-and-slice-chart",
          "def-diffeomorphism-and-local-diffeomorphism-of-manifolds",
          "def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair",
          "def-stable-leaf-of-a-foliation",
          "cor-codimension-one-frobenius-criterion",
          "thm-frobenius-local-coordinate-theorem"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]]). Let\n$X:=\\overline D^2\\times S^1$ be the solid torus, where\n$\\overline D^2=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x^2+y^2\\le1\\}$\n([[def-euclidean-spheres-and-closed-balls]]) and $\\partial X\\cong S^1\\times S^1$\nis the boundary torus ([[def-two-dimensional-torus]]). Define\n$u:\\operatorname{Int}\\overline D^2\\to(0,\\infty)$ by\n$u(r):=\\exp\\bigl(1/(1-r^2)\\bigr)$ for $0\\le r<1$, with $r=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$, and\nconsider the level sets of the submersion $f(x,y,t)=u(r)-t$ on\n$\\operatorname{Int}\\overline D^2\\times\\mathbb R$; add the boundary $\\partial X$\nas a leaf. This defines a codimension-one regular foliation\n$F_{\\mathrm{Reeb}}$ of $X$ tangent to $\\partial X$\n([[def-foliation-tangent-to-the-boundary-of-a-manifold-with-boundary]]), and:\n\n1. the boundary $\\partial X$ is a compact leaf diffeomorphic to $T^2$;\n2. every other leaf is diffeomorphic to $\\mathbb R^2$ and accumulates on the\n   boundary leaf;\n3. the foliation is invariant under the translation $t\\mapsto t+1$, so it\n   descends to the quotient $X=\\overline D^2\\times\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$;\n4. the holonomy group of the boundary leaf is infinite: the holonomy of the\n   loop in the $S^1$-factor through a boundary point is represented by the germ\n   of the contraction $r\\mapsto r'$ determined by $u(r')=u(r)+1$, a\n   non-identity germ of a one-sided interval; consequently the boundary leaf is\n   compact but has infinite holonomy and is not stable\n   ([[def-stable-leaf-of-a-foliation]]).",
        "strategy": "The function $f$ is a submersion on $\\operatorname{Int}\\overline D^2\\times\\mathbb R$ with nowhere-vanishing differential because $\\partial_t f=-1$; its level sets are smooth surfaces of revolution, and [[cor-kernel-of-a-constant-rank-submersion-is-integrable]] integrates the kernel distribution, giving a regular codimension-one foliation of the open solid cylinder whose leaves are the level sets. Each level set is the graph of $t=u(r)-c$ over the punctured disk, hence diffeomorphic to the once-punctured disk times $S^1$, i.e. to $\\mathbb R^2$; it accumulates on $\\{r=1\\}\\cong T^2$ because $u(r)\\to\\infty$ and the spiral wraps infinitely often. Adding the boundary as a leaf is the boundary-tangency condition of [[def-foliation-tangent-to-the-boundary-of-a-manifold-with-boundary]]; a half-space chart at a boundary point is obtained from $(r,\\theta,t)$ with $s=1-r\\ge0$. The translation $t\\mapsto t+1$ preserves the level sets, is a covering-space action on the open cylinder, and [[prop-quotient-foliation-under-a-free-proper-foliated-action]] descends the foliation to $X$; the quotient of the boundary circle is again a circle. The holonomy computation is the transport of a radial transversal through one turn of the $S^1$ factor: the leaf through $(r,\\theta,0)$ reaches the same transversal at time $1$ at the unique $r'$ with $u(r')=u(r)+1$; since $u$ is strictly increasing this $r'$ satisfies $r'>r$ (the map is nontrivial), and $r'\\to1$ as $r\\to1$, so the germ is a one-sided contraction. Hence the holonomy group contains a non-identity element and is infinite, and stability fails by [[def-stable-leaf-of-a-foliation]]. Only the standing $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is used.\n\nHere $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ denotes the countable-choice principle [[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]].",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "literature-derived"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Danny Calegari, Foliations and the Geometry of 3-Manifolds (Oxford Mathematical Monographs; complete author-hosted PDF)",
              "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/books/foliations/oupbook.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a74.3, Example 4.7, printed pp. 144\u2013145 (the Reeb component as the quotient of the upper half-space minus the origin)"
            },
            {
              "title": "Leiden NCG seminar, Noncommutative Geometry of Foliations (2023 seminar notes; complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://ncg-leiden.github.io/foliation2023/foliation_notes.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a72.1\u2013\u00a72.2, printed pp. 11\u201315 (Reeb foliation of the solid torus and its contracting holonomy)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "rem-transverse-orientability-is-load-bearing-in-the-global-codimension-one-form",
        "kind": "remark",
        "title": "Transverse orientability is load-bearing in the global codimension-one form",
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair",
          "def-transversely-oriented-codimension-one-foliation"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume the standing countable choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]]); the following finite quotient construction needs no further choice. On $S^2\\times S^1$, with $\\theta\\in\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$, consider $\\tau(x,\\theta)=(-x,-\\theta)$. This involution is free, because the antipodal map on $S^2$ has no fixed point. Small disjoint neighborhoods of a point and its image give smooth quotient charts, so $X=(S^2\\times S^1)/\\langle\\tau\\rangle$ is a closed connected smooth three-manifold.\n\nThe product foliation descends. For $\\theta\\ne0,1/2$ a pair of slices at $\\theta,-\\theta$ has image diffeomorphic to $S^2$. At $0$ and $1/2$ the slice is identified antipodally and its image is $\\mathbb RP^2$. All these leaves are compact and have finite fundamental group. The leaf space is the quotient of the circle by reflection, hence a closed interval, with the two projective-plane leaves at its endpoints.\n\nThe descended foliation is not transversely orientable ([[def-transversely-oriented-codimension-one-foliation]]). Indeed a hypothetical nonzero coorientation would pull back to $a(x,\\theta)\\,d\\theta$ on the connected product, where $a$ is a continuous nowhere-zero function. Invariance under $\\tau$ requires $a(-x,-\\theta)=-a(x,\\theta)$, impossible because a continuous nowhere-zero real function on a connected space has constant sign. Thus the common-leaf and circle-fibration conclusions of global Reeb stability fail when transverse orientability is removed. Under full AC this is a counterexample to removing just that hypothesis from the global theorem, rather than an application of its proof under countable choice alone.",
        "strategy": "The involution is free because $(-x,-\\theta)=(x,\\theta)$ would force $x=-x$ on $S^2$; the quotient is therefore a closed manifold. The foliation and its leaves are computed as in Mrowka, Remark 7: the image of a slice $S^2\\times\\{\\theta\\}$ is a sphere for generic $\\theta$ and a projective plane for the two $\\theta$-classes fixed by $\\theta\\mapsto-\\theta$, and the normal bundle of the descended foliation is the quotient of the trivial co-orientation line by the $-1$ action of $\\tau$, hence non-orientable. The failure of the global conclusion is therefore a genuine failure of the hypothesis, not of the proof. No choice principle beyond the standing $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is used.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Tomasz Mrowka, MIT 18.965 Differential Topology, lecture notes (complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://math.mit.edu/~mrowka/math965lectnote.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a7\u00a720\u201323, PDF pp. 52\u201356"
            },
            {
              "title": "David Gabai, Commentary on Foliations (in Collected Works of William P. Thurston, Vol. 1; author-hosted)",
              "url": "https://web.math.princeton.edu/facultypapers/Gabai/Commentary-Thurston-Foliations.pdf",
              "locator": "Theorem 0.8 and Remark 0.9, PDF pp. 2\u20133"
            },
            {
              "title": "Danny Calegari, Foliations and the Geometry of 3-Manifolds (Oxford Mathematical Monographs; complete author-hosted PDF)",
              "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/books/foliations/oupbook.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a74.2, printed pp. 140\u2013143 (PDF pp. 149\u2013152); \u00a74.3, printed pp. 144\u2013145 (PDF pp. 153\u2013154), Example 4.7; Lemma 4.24, printed p. 155 (PDF p. 164)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "external_refs": [
          "thm-global-reeb-stability-for-transversely-oriented-codimension-one-foliations"
        ],
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-thurston-stability-for-c1-interval-germ-groups-are-locally-indicable",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "Thurston stability: finitely generated groups of C\u00b9 interval germs are locally indicable",
        "deps": [
          "def-c1-germ-of-a-local-diffeomorphism-at-a-point",
          "lem-c1-germs-of-local-diffeomorphisms-form-a-group",
          "def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse",
          "def-group",
          "def-subgroup",
          "cor-fundamental-theorem-of-finitely-generated-abelian-groups-from-pid-modules",
          "lem-images-of-finitely-generated-and-finite-groups-are-finitely-generated-and-finite"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $G$ be a nontrivial finitely generated subgroup of\n$\\operatorname{Diff}^{1,+}_0(\\mathbb R)$, the group of germs at $0$ of $C^1$\norientation-preserving local diffeomorphisms of $\\mathbb R$ fixing $0$\n([[def-c1-germ-of-a-local-diffeomorphism-at-a-point]],\n[[lem-c1-germs-of-local-diffeomorphisms-form-a-group]]). Then there is a\nsurjective homomorphism $G\\twoheadrightarrow\\mathbb Z$; that is,\n$\\operatorname{Diff}^{1,+}_0(\\mathbb R)$ is locally indicable.",
        "strategy": "The group operation is well defined by [[lem-c1-germs-of-local-diffeomorphisms-form-a-group]], and the $C^1$ local-diffeomorphism terminology is [[def-c-one-map-and-local-inverse]], [[def-group]] and [[def-subgroup]]. Use the derivative homomorphism $d(g)=\\log g'(0)$. If $d|_G$ is nonzero, its image is a nonzero finitely generated torsion-free abelian subgroup of $\\mathbb R$; the classification of finitely generated abelian groups ([[cor-fundamental-theorem-of-finitely-generated-abelian-groups-from-pid-modules]]) makes it $\\mathbb Z^r$ with $r\\ge1$, and projection onto one factor gives a surjection to $\\mathbb Z$. If $d|_G=0$, choose generators $g_1,\\ldots,g_m$. Unless $G$ is trivial, some generator moves points arbitrarily close to $0$ on at least one side; work on that side and choose $x_n\\to0$ with $w_n=\\max_j|g_j(x_n)-x_n|>0$. The normalized displacement vectors $a_n=((g_j(x_n)-x_n)/w_n)_j$ lie in the compact cube $[-1,1]^m$ with maximum norm $1$; a convergent subsequence has limit $a$ with maximum norm $1$. Since $g_j'(0)=1$, $w_n=o(|x_n|)$. For each fixed word, induction over its letters shows each partial word moves $x_n$ by $O(w_n)$; continuity of the first derivatives makes the displacement of the next generator at that point differ from its displacement at $x_n$ by $o(w_n)$. The mean-value theorem first bounds inverse-letter displacement by $O(w_n)$ and then gives its normalized contribution as the negative generator coordinate. Thus every fixed word has normalized displacement converging to its signed exponent sum paired with $a$. A relation has identically zero displacement near $0$, so its limiting exponent sum is zero. The limit therefore defines a homomorphism $G\\to\\mathbb R$, nonzero because some coordinate of $a$ has absolute value $1$. Its image is a nonzero finitely generated torsion-free abelian group, hence has a quotient $\\mathbb Z$. This expands the compressed estimates in Calegari's proof; only $C^1$ regularity, specifically continuity of the first derivative, is used.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Danny Calegari, Foliations and the Geometry of 3-Manifolds (Oxford Mathematical Monographs; complete author-hosted PDF)",
              "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/books/foliations/oupbook.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a72.16.2, Theorem 2.119 (Thurston stability theorem) and its complete proof, printed pp. 106\u2013107 (PDF pp. 115\u2013116); the proof uses only C\u00b9 regularity"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-compact-c1-leaf-has-finitely-generated-fundamental-group",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "A compact C\u00b9 leaf has finitely generated fundamental group",
        "deps": [
          "def-c1-regular-codimension-one-foliation-and-transverse-orientation",
          "thm-smooth-partitions-of-unity-exist-on-manifolds",
          "def-mollifier-family-generated-by-a-unit-mass-smooth-bump",
          "thm-a-regular-level-set-is-an-embedded-submanifold",
          "thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-a-maximal-ode-solution",
          "lem-a-closed-smooth-manifold-has-the-homotopy-type-of-a-finite-cw-complex",
          "def-cw-complex-with-closure-finiteness-and-weak-topology",
          "thm-seifert-van-kampen",
          "thm-higher-dimensional-spheres-are-simply-connected",
          "def-based-loops-and-fundamental-group",
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem",
          "lem-compact-c1-foliation-leaf-is-an-embedded-hypersurface",
          "lem-axiom-of-choice-implies-countable-choice"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). If $L$ is a compact\nconnected leaf of a transversely oriented $C^1$ codimension-one foliation of a\nsmooth manifold, then $\\pi_1(L,x)$ is finitely generated for every $x\\in L$\n([[def-based-loops-and-fundamental-group]]).",
        "strategy": "The compact leaf is an embedded C\u00b9 hypersurface by [[lem-compact-c1-foliation-leaf-is-an-embedded-hypersurface]]. The transverse coordinates of foliation charts give local C\u00b9 defining functions $f_i$ whose differentials along $L$ are positive multiples of the coorientation conormal; their chart neighborhoods cover $L$, so compactness gives a finite subcover. Choose smaller neighborhoods with closures inside these charts and a smooth partition of unity subordinate to them ([[thm-smooth-partitions-of-unity-exist-on-manifolds]]). The weighted sum $f=\\sum_i\\chi_i f_i$, with supported terms extended by zero, is C\u00b9 and vanishes on $L$. At each $p\\in L$, its differential is $df_p=\\sum_i\\chi_i(p)d(f_i)_p$, a positive coorientation conormal because every active $d(f_i)_p$ is a positive multiple of that conormal; hence $df_p\\ne0$. For each $p\\in L$ such a positive local field exists on a neighborhood of $p$; these neighborhoods cover $L$. Compactness gives a finite subcover, and a smooth partition of unity subordinate to it combines the corresponding fields into a smooth vector field $V$ with $df(V)>0$ on $L$. Compactness gives $df(V)>c>0$ on a smaller neighborhood. The smooth flow exists there for a uniform time ([[thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-a-maximal-ode-solution]]). The map $(p,t)\\mapsto\\Phi_t(p)$ has invertible derivative at every $(p,0)$, since $T_pL\\oplus\\mathbb RV(p)=T_pM$; the C\u00b9 inverse function theorem and compactness give a uniform flow collar after shrinking. For the global injectivity step, if no common time interval were injective, distinct $(p_j,t_j),(q_j,s_j)$ with $t_j,s_j\\to0$ and equal images would have a subsequence converging to the same $(p,0)$, contradicting local injectivity there. Along each flow segment $f$ is strictly increasing from $0$, so this collar also shows $f^{-1}(0)=L$. Smooth $f$ in C\u00b9 on a compact subcollar by a finite-chart mollifier argument: decompose it using a finite smooth partition, extend each supported coordinate function by zero to Euclidean space, convolve with a standard mollifier ([[def-mollifier-family-generated-by-a-unit-mass-smooth-bump]]), and use uniform continuity of both the function and its first derivatives on the compact supports to obtain uniform convergence of the functions and their first derivatives. The finite sum is smooth and can be made arbitrarily C\u00b9-close to $f$. Let $\\widetilde f$ be close enough that $d\\widetilde f(V)>c/2$ and its values at the two ends of each flow segment have opposite signs. The smooth flow of $V$ and monotonicity in the flow parameter give exactly one zero of $\\widetilde f$ on each segment; the C\u00b9 implicit function theorem ([[thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem]]) makes that zero a C\u00b9 function of the basepoint. Thus the nearby zero set is a smooth compact hypersurface homeomorphic to $L$ and is a regular level set ([[thm-a-regular-level-set-is-an-embedded-submanifold]]). Apply [[lem-a-closed-smooth-manifold-has-the-homotopy-type-of-a-finite-cw-complex]] to this smooth zero set. A finite CW model has a finite connected $1$-skeleton, whose fundamental group is finitely generated; attaching its finitely many $2$-cells takes a quotient by [[thm-seifert-van-kampen]], and cells of dimension at least $3$ do not change $\\pi_1$ because their attaching spheres are simply connected ([[thm-higher-dimensional-spheres-are-simply-connected]]). The homeomorphism transfers finite generation to $\\pi_1(L,x)$ ([[def-based-loops-and-fundamental-group]]). The finite-CW supplier uses full AC, and its countable-choice hypothesis is derived locally from AC by [[lem-axiom-of-choice-implies-countable-choice]] ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]).",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "John Milnor, Morse Theory (Annals of Mathematics Studies 51; complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://www.maths.ed.ac.uk/~v1ranick/papers/milnmors.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a73, Theorem 3.5, printed pp. 20\u201321 (finite CW homotopy type); the C\u00b9 smoothing and the flow comparison are proved locally"
            },
            {
              "title": "David Gabai, Commentary on Thurston's Foliations and the Thurston norm",
              "url": "https://web.math.princeton.edu/facultypapers/Gabai/Commentary-Thurston-Foliations.pdf",
              "locator": "Theorem 0.8, PDF p. 2 (compact C\u00b9 leaf setting)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-rational-homology-of-a-closed-smooth-manifold-is-finite-dimensional",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "The rational homology of a closed smooth manifold is finite-dimensional in each degree",
        "deps": [
          "lem-a-closed-smooth-manifold-has-the-homotopy-type-of-a-finite-cw-complex",
          "def-cw-complex-with-closure-finiteness-and-weak-topology",
          "def-cellular-homology",
          "thm-cellular-homology-computes-singular-homology",
          "def-singular-chain-complex-and-singular-homology",
          "lem-subgroups-of-free-abelian-groups-are-free",
          "def-smooth-manifold",
          "def-compact-space",
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair",
          "thm-homotopy-equivalences-induce-isomorphisms-on-singular-homology"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]]) and the Axiom of Choice\nas consumed by [[lem-a-closed-smooth-manifold-has-the-homotopy-type-of-a-finite-cw-complex]]\nand [[lem-subgroups-of-free-abelian-groups-are-free]] ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]).\nLet $M$ be a closed smooth manifold ([[def-smooth-manifold]],\n[[def-compact-space]]). Then $H_k(M;\\mathbb Z)$ is finitely generated for every\n$k$, and the rational vector space $H_k(M;\\mathbb Q)$ is finite-dimensional. In\nparticular $H_2(M;\\mathbb Q)$ is finite-dimensional, and every subspace of\n$H_k(M;\\mathbb Q)$ generated by countably many homology classes is\nfinite-dimensional.",
        "strategy": "By [[lem-a-closed-smooth-manifold-has-the-homotopy-type-of-a-finite-cw-complex]] $M$ has the homotopy type of a finite CW complex $X$, and a homotopy equivalence induces isomorphisms on homology with every coefficient group. Since $X$ has finitely many cells, its cellular chain groups are finitely generated and free over $\\mathbb Z$, hence finite-dimensional over $\\mathbb Q$ after tensoring, and [[thm-cellular-homology-computes-singular-homology]] identifies $H_*(X;G)$ with the homology of the cellular complex with coefficients $G$ for every abelian group $G$. With $G=\\mathbb Q$ this exhibits $H_k(M;\\mathbb Q)$ as a quotient of a subspace of a finite-dimensional $\\mathbb Q$-vector space, hence finite-dimensional. With $G=\\mathbb Z$: the cycle group $Z_k$ is a subgroup of a finitely generated free abelian group, hence free and finitely generated ([[lem-subgroups-of-free-abelian-groups-are-free]]), the boundary group $B_k$ is a quotient of a finitely generated abelian group, hence finitely generated, and therefore $H_k(M;\\mathbb Z)\\cong Z_k/B_k$ is finitely generated. Finally a subspace of a finite-dimensional space that is spanned by countably many vectors is spanned by finitely many of them. Only the standing $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, the axiom of choice used by [[lem-subgroups-of-free-abelian-groups-are-free]] and the choice consumed by [[lem-a-closed-smooth-manifold-has-the-homotopy-type-of-a-finite-cw-complex]] are used.\n\nHere $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ denotes the countable-choice principle [[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]].",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "literature-derived"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "John Milnor, Morse Theory (Annals of Mathematics Studies 51; complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://www.maths.ed.ac.uk/~v1ranick/papers/milnmors.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a73, printed pp. 17\u201321, Theorem 3.5 (a Morse function yields a CW homotopy type with one cell per critical point; finitely many cells for a compact manifold); \u00a76 (existence of Morse functions on compact manifolds)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-trivial-c1-holonomy-gives-a-saturated-product-neighbourhood",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Trivial C\u00b9 holonomy gives a saturated product neighbourhood",
        "deps": [
          "lem-chart-bump-at-a-point-with-prescribed-support",
          "thm-fundamental-theorem-on-flows",
          "thm-intermediate-value",
          "def-c1-regular-codimension-one-foliation-and-transverse-orientation",
          "lem-c1-holonomy-is-a-well-defined-representation-into-transverse-germs",
          "def-compact-space",
          "def-saturated-neighbourhood-of-a-leaf",
          "def-smooth-manifold",
          "thm-euclidean-inverse-function-theorem",
          "def-embedded-submanifold-and-slice-chart",
          "lem-compact-c1-foliation-leaf-is-an-embedded-hypersurface",
          "thm-finite-products-of-compact-spaces",
          "cor-euclidean-closed-balls-and-spheres-are-compact",
          "thm-compact-iff-fip",
          "cor-the-agreement-set-of-two-maps-into-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed",
          "def-topological-manifold-without-boundary",
          "def-hausdorff-space"
        ],
        "statement": "Let $F$ be a transversely oriented $C^1$ codimension-one foliation of a smooth\nmanifold $M$, and let $L$ be a compact leaf with trivial $C^1$ holonomy\n([[lem-c1-holonomy-is-a-well-defined-representation-into-transverse-germs]]).\nThen there are an open interval $D$ and a saturated open neighbourhood $U$ of\n$L$ with a $C^1$ foliated diffeomorphism\n$$(U,F|_U)\\cong\\bigl(L\\times D,\\ \\{L\\times\\{t\\}\\}_{t\\in D}\\bigr),$$\nthat is, a $C^1$ diffeomorphism carrying the foliation $F|_U$ onto the product\nfoliation by the slices.",
        "strategy": "Prove compact injectivity for a local C1 diffeomorphism equal to inclusion on the central slice. Normalize finitely many plaque-chart first integrals by actual positive path-transport representatives. Construct a smooth positively transverse field from finitely many chart bumps and a C1 flow collar. Trivial loop germs give equality of normalized first integrals on ambient neighborhoods of each overlap point; compact overlap closures and finitely many pairs give one actual common collar domain. The resulting global C1 first integral has positive collar derivative. Uniform endpoint bounds and the inverse function theorem construct \u03a6=(P,r)^-1 on L\u00d7D. Each slice is compact, open and closed in its intrinsic connected Hausdorff leaf, hence the full leaf; the image is saturated. All selections are finite, with no new AC hypothesis.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Danny Calegari, Foliations and the Geometry of 3-Manifolds (Oxford Mathematical Monographs; complete author-hosted PDF)",
              "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/books/foliations/oupbook.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a74.2, printed pp. 140\u2013143 (holonomy transport and the product neighbourhood of a leaf with trivial holonomy)"
            },
            {
              "title": "David Gabai, Commentary on Thurston's Foliations and the Thurston norm",
              "url": "https://web.math.princeton.edu/facultypapers/Gabai/Commentary-Thurston-Foliations.pdf",
              "locator": "Theorem 0.8(b), PDF p. 2 (local stability conclusion)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "prop-gluing-two-reeb-components-gives-a-foliation-of-s-three",
        "kind": "proposition",
        "title": "Gluing two Reeb components gives a foliation of the three-sphere",
        "deps": [
          "prop-reeb-foliation-of-the-solid-torus-has-the-boundary-as-a-leaf",
          "lem-gluing-manifolds-with-boundary-along-a-boundary-diffeomorphism",
          "def-euclidean-spheres-and-closed-balls",
          "def-two-dimensional-torus",
          "def-diffeomorphism-and-local-diffeomorphism-of-manifolds",
          "def-smooth-embedding",
          "def-foliation-tangent-to-the-boundary-of-a-manifold-with-boundary",
          "def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]]). Let $X_1,X_2$ be two\ncopies of the solid torus $\\overline D^2\\times S^1$ with their Reeb foliations\n([[prop-reeb-foliation-of-the-solid-torus-has-the-boundary-as-a-leaf]]) and let\n$\\sigma:\\partial X_1\\to\\partial X_2$ be the diffeomorphism which interchanges\nthe two circle factors, written in the boundary coordinates\n$\\partial X_j\\cong S^1\\times S^1$ as $\\sigma(u,v)=(v,u)$. Then the glued\nmanifold $X_1\\cup_\\sigma X_2$ is diffeomorphic to the three-sphere $S^3$\n([[def-euclidean-spheres-and-closed-balls]]), the standard genus-one splitting,\nand the two Reeb foliations glue by\n[[lem-gluing-manifolds-with-boundary-along-a-boundary-diffeomorphism]] to a\ncodimension-one regular foliation $F_{\\mathrm{Reeb}}$ of $S^3$. This foliation\nhas exactly one compact leaf, the Heegaard torus\n$\\partial X_1=\\partial X_2$\n([[def-two-dimensional-torus]]), whose holonomy group is infinite; every other\nleaf is diffeomorphic to $\\mathbb R^2$ and accumulates on the torus leaf. Hence\na compact manifold can carry a codimension-one foliation with non-compact\nleaves and a single unstable compact leaf.",
        "strategy": "Realize $S^3=\\{(z_1,z_2)\\in\\mathbb C^2:|z_1|^2+|z_2|^2=1\\}$ and write it as the union of the two closed solid tori $V_1=\\{|z_1|^2\\le\\frac12\\}$ and $V_2=\\{|z_2|^2\\le\\frac12\\}$ meeting along the torus $\\{|z_1|=|z_2|=1/\\sqrt2\\}$. The maps $V_1\\to\\overline D^2\\times S^1$, $(z_1,z_2)\\mapsto(\\sqrt2\\,z_1,z_2/|z_2|)$, and $V_2\\to\\overline D^2\\times S^1$, $(z_1,z_2)\\mapsto(\\sqrt2\\,z_2,z_1/|z_1|)$, are diffeomorphisms (the second coordinate is well defined since the other coordinate is nonzero on each closed piece), and on the common boundary torus the transition is $(u,v)\\mapsto(v,u)=\\sigma$. Thus $S^3\\cong X_1\\cup_\\sigma X_2$ with the stated gluing map, and the foliation claim is the gluing lemma applied to the Reeb foliations, which are tangent to the boundary with the boundary torus as a single leaf on each side, so the compatibility hypothesis of the gluing lemma holds automatically. The unique compact leaf and the accumulation behaviour are inherited from [[prop-reeb-foliation-of-the-solid-torus-has-the-boundary-as-a-leaf]]; a non-compact leaf of the glued foliation lies in one of the two Reeb components, so it is a plane, and the torus leaf retains its infinite holonomy. The statement uses the standard description of the genus-one Heegaard splitting, which is proved here by the explicit formulas. Only the standing $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is used.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "literature-derived"
        },
        "sources": {
