      {
        "id": "thm-reeb-thurston-stability-for-codimension-one-leaves",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "Reeb-Thurston stability for codimension-one leaves with vanishing first real cohomology",
        "deps": [
          "def-c1-regular-codimension-one-foliation-and-transverse-orientation",
          "def-saturated-neighbourhood-of-a-leaf",
          "def-singular-cohomology-with-coefficients",
          "def-compact-space",
          "lem-c1-holonomy-is-a-well-defined-representation-into-transverse-germs",
          "lem-compact-c1-leaf-has-finitely-generated-fundamental-group",
          "thm-thurston-stability-for-c1-interval-germ-groups-are-locally-indicable",
          "lem-trivial-c1-holonomy-gives-a-saturated-product-neighbourhood",
          "def-singular-chain-complex-and-singular-homology",
          "def-singular-cochain-complex-with-coefficients",
          "prop-the-first-hurewicz-map-in-degree-one-is-abelianization",
          "thm-universal-coefficient-theorem-for-cohomology-over-a-pid",
          "prop-zero-th-singular-homology-is-free-on-path-components",
          "thm-free-modules-are-projective-with-choice-boundary",
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "lem-images-of-finitely-generated-and-finite-groups-are-finitely-generated-and-finite"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $F$ be a $C^1$\ntransversely oriented codimension-one foliation of a smooth manifold $M$, and\nlet $L$ be a compact leaf with $H^1(L;\\mathbb R)=0$\n([[def-singular-cohomology-with-coefficients]]). Then $L$ has a saturated open\nneighbourhood whose leaves are all diffeomorphic to $L$. In fact the holonomy\nof $L$ is trivial and the neighbourhood can be chosen to be a product foliated\nneighbourhood $L\\times D$ for an open interval $D$. This is the local\ncohomological refinement; no global fibration conclusion is asserted.",
        "strategy": "Assume AC and fix $x\\in L$. By [[lem-compact-c1-leaf-has-finitely-generated-fundamental-group]], $\\pi_1(L,x)$ is finitely generated. Apply the degree-one Hurewicz theorem and the cohomological universal-coefficient sequence to the singular chain and cochain complexes ([[def-singular-chain-complex-and-singular-homology]], [[def-singular-cochain-complex-with-coefficients]], [[def-singular-cohomology-with-coefficients]], [[prop-the-first-hurewicz-map-in-degree-one-is-abelianization]], [[thm-universal-coefficient-theorem-for-cohomology-over-a-pid]]). Since $L$ is connected, $H_0(L;\\mathbb Z)\\cong\\mathbb Z$ ([[prop-zero-th-singular-homology-is-free-on-path-components]]); this free module is projective ([[thm-free-modules-are-projective-with-choice-boundary]]), so the Ext term in degree one vanishes. Hence $H^1(L;\\mathbb R)\\cong\\operatorname{Hom}(\\pi_1(L,x),\\mathbb R)$, and the hypothesis makes every real character trivial. By [[lem-c1-holonomy-is-a-well-defined-representation-into-transverse-germs]], the holonomy image is a finitely generated subgroup of orientation-preserving C\u00b9 interval germs. If it were nontrivial, [[thm-thurston-stability-for-c1-interval-germ-groups-are-locally-indicable]] would give a surjection of that image onto $\\mathbb Z$; composing with holonomy and $\\mathbb Z\\hookrightarrow\\mathbb R$ would yield a nonzero real character of $\\pi_1(L,x)$, a contradiction. Holonomy is therefore trivial, and [[lem-trivial-c1-holonomy-gives-a-saturated-product-neighbourhood]] gives the saturated product neighbourhood and diffeomorphic leaves. AC is used by the published universal-coefficient and finite-CW suppliers and is declared in the statement.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Danny Calegari, Foliations and the Geometry of 3-Manifolds (Oxford Mathematical Monographs; complete author-hosted PDF)",
              "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/books/foliations/oupbook.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a72.16.2, Theorem 2.119 and its complete proof, printed pp. 106\u2013107 (the C\u00b9 local-indicability input)"
            },
            {
              "title": "David Gabai, Commentary on Thurston's Foliations and the Thurston norm",
              "url": "https://web.math.princeton.edu/facultypapers/Gabai/Commentary-Thurston-Foliations.pdf",
              "locator": "Theorem 0.8(a),(b), PDF p. 2 (the C\u00b9 cohomological global and local forms)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 4,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-finite-holonomy-acts-on-a-small-transverse-disk",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Finite holonomy acts on a small transverse disk",
        "deps": [
          "def-holonomy-representation-and-holonomy-group-of-a-leaf",
          "def-local-transversal-to-a-regular-foliation",
          "def-germ-of-a-local-diffeomorphism-at-a-point",
          "lem-germs-of-local-diffeomorphisms-form-a-group",
          "def-embedded-submanifold-and-slice-chart",
          "def-smooth-manifold",
          "def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]]). Let $F$ be a regular\nfoliation, $L$ a leaf, $x\\in L$, and $T$ a local transversal to $F$ at $x$\nchosen to be an embedded open disk\n([[def-local-transversal-to-a-regular-foliation]]). Suppose\n$H=\\operatorname{Hol}(L,x)\\le\\operatorname{Diff}_x(T)$ is finite\n([[def-holonomy-representation-and-holonomy-group-of-a-leaf]]). Then there is\nan $H$-invariant open neighbourhood $D\\subseteq T$ of $x$ such that:\n\n1. every $h\\in H$ has a representative diffeomorphism defined on $D$ with\n   $h(D)=D$, and these representatives make $H$ act on $D$ by diffeomorphisms\n   restricting the given germs;\n2. each $h\\in H$ extends to a diffeomorphism defined on a neighbourhood of the\n   closure of $D$;\n3. if in addition the finitely many germs preserve a metric germ on $T$, the\n   disk $D$ may be taken to be an open metric ball.\n\nAny open $H$-invariant $D$ suffices for the finite-holonomy normal model.",
        "strategy": "Since $H$ is finite, first choose representatives and restrict their domains so every finitely many group-law equality $g\\circ h=gh$ holds on one neighborhood, and all representative images of a smaller neighborhood lie inside every composition domain. Then choose representatives $h:U_h\\to T$ of the finitely many elements of $H$ and of their inverses on a common open neighbourhood $U$ of $x$ (a finite intersection of neighbourhoods). The finite intersection $D_0:=\\bigcap_{h\\in H}h(U)$ is an open neighbourhood of $x$, and for $g\\in H$ one has $g(D_0)=\\bigcap_{h\\in H}gh(U)=\\bigcap_{k\\in H}k(U)=D_0$ because $gH=H$; moreover each $g|_{D_0}$ is a diffeomorphism of $D_0$ restricting the given germ. For (ii), choose the common domain $U$ relatively compact in $T$; then $D$ can be shrunk so that the closure of $D$ is still contained in $U$, whence each representative extends to a neighbourhood of the closure. For (iii), if the germs preserve a metric germ, average the metric over the finite group to obtain an invariant metric germ and take a small metric ball. The germ group structure supplies the compatibility of representatives on overlaps. The construction is finite and uses only the standing $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$.\n\nHere $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ denotes the countable-choice principle [[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]].",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "literature-derived"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Ieke Moerdijk and Janez Mr\u010dun, Introduction to Foliations and Lie Groupoids (Cambridge Studies in Advanced Mathematics 91, 2003)",
              "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/introduction-to-foliations-and-lie-groupoids/75BBFED277FF39A56594731202789016",
              "locator": "Design locators: \u00a72.3, pp. 30\u201333 (local Reeb stability)"
            },
            {
              "title": "Leiden NCG seminar, Noncommutative Geometry of Foliations (2023 seminar notes; complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://ncg-leiden.github.io/foliation2023/foliation_notes.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a72.1\u2013\u00a72.2, printed pp. 11\u201315 (germs of transverse diffeomorphisms and finite holonomy)"
            },
            {
              "title": "A. Candel and L. Conlon-type local Reeb stability note (arXiv:1204.0095)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1204.0095",
              "locator": "\u00a7\u00a71\u20132, PDF pp. 1\u20133 (tubular form of the local Reeb stability theorem)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 5,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-deck-group-of-the-holonomy-cover-is-the-holonomy-group",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "The deck group of the holonomy cover is the holonomy group",
        "deps": [
          "def-holonomy-cover-of-a-leaf",
          "def-holonomy-representation-and-holonomy-group-of-a-leaf",
          "lem-the-covering-of-a-leaf-associated-to-the-holonomy-kernel-exists",
          "lem-the-deck-group-of-a-covering-acts-by-a-covering-space-action",
          "def-deck-transformation-and-deck-group",
          "def-covering-map-and-evenly-covered-neighbourhoods",
          "thm-path-lifting-for-covering-maps",
          "thm-homotopy-lifting-for-covering-maps",
          "thm-uniqueness-of-lifts-from-a-connected-space",
          "thm-covering-maps-inject-fundamental-groups",
          "prop-deck-transformations-are-determined-by-one-point-and-act-freely",
          "def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair",
          "def-induced-homomorphism-on-fundamental-groups",
          "thm-deck-group-of-a-universal-cover-is-the-fundamental-group",
          "cor-deck-group-of-a-regular-covering",
          "thm-first-isomorphism-theorem-groups"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]]). Let $F$ be a regular\nfoliation, $L$ a leaf, $x\\in L$, $T$ a local transversal at $x$,\n$\\rho_x:\\pi_1(L,x)\\to\\operatorname{Diff}_x(T)$ the holonomy representation and\n$p:\\widehat L\\to L$ the holonomy cover, with $\\hat x\\in p^{-1}(x)$ and\n$p_*\\pi_1(\\widehat L,\\hat x)=\\ker\\rho_x$\n([[def-holonomy-cover-of-a-leaf]]). Then:\n\n1. $p$ is a regular covering, $\\pi_1(L,x)/\\ker\\rho_x$ acts faithfully on\n   $\\widehat L$ by deck transformations, and\n   $\\operatorname{Deck}(p)\\cong\\pi_1(L,x)/\\ker\\rho_x\\cong\\operatorname{Hol}(L,x)=\\rho_x(\\pi_1(L,x))$;\n2. the deck action is a covering-space action,\n   $\\widehat L/\\operatorname{Deck}(p)\\cong L$, and the quotient map is $p$;\n3. the holonomy group acts through transverse germs via $\\rho_x$; when those\n   germs are realized on a common invariant transverse neighbourhood $D$, the\n   diagonal action of $H=\\operatorname{Hol}(L,x)$ on $\\widehat L\\times D$ is\n   free and a covering-space action. In particular, when $H$ is finite, $p$ is\n   a finite-sheeted covering of degree $|H|$.",
        "strategy": "Use the path-class construction of the holonomy cover with $N=\\ker\\rho_x$: a point over $y$ is represented by a path $a$ from $x$ to $y$, with representatives identified when the resulting based loop lies in $N$. For a based loop $\\gamma$ at $x$, prepend $\\gamma$ to $a$; normality of $N$ makes this independent of both representatives. On lifted simply connected plaque neighborhoods this is a smooth deck map, and prepending $\\gamma^{-1}$ is its inverse. Traversal-order concatenation gives $\\tau_\\gamma\\tau_\\delta=\\tau_{\\gamma*\\delta}$, so the kernel is $N$ and the action on each fibre is transitive and free. Uniqueness of lifts shows every deck map is obtained this way. Thus $\\operatorname{Deck}(p)\\cong\\pi_1(L,x)/N\\cong\\operatorname{Hol}(L,x)$, and the covering-space-action lemma gives the quotient $\\widehat L/\\operatorname{Deck}(p)\\cong L$. Holonomy elements initially act as transverse germs; when $H$ is finite, the next lemma realizes them on one invariant neighborhood $D$. The diagonal action there is free and a covering-space action because the first factor is one. Its fibres have $|H|$ points. Each deck map is defined on path classes, without selecting a representative for every class; only the standing $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ foliation interface is used.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "literature-derived"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Leiden NCG seminar, Noncommutative Geometry of Foliations (2023 seminar notes; complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://ncg-leiden.github.io/foliation2023/foliation_notes.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a72.1\u2013\u00a72.2, printed pp. 11\u201315 (the holonomy cover and its deck group)"
            },
            {
              "title": "Ieke Moerdijk and Janez Mr\u010dun, Introduction to Foliations and Lie Groupoids (Cambridge Studies in Advanced Mathematics 91, 2003)",
              "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/introduction-to-foliations-and-lie-groupoids/75BBFED277FF39A56594731202789016",
              "locator": "Design locators: \u00a72.3, pp. 30\u201333; \u00a72.5, pp. 44\u201351 (the holonomy cover and the normal model)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 7,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "def-finite-holonomy-normal-model",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "The finite-holonomy normal model of a compact leaf",
        "deps": [
          "def-saturated-neighbourhood-of-a-leaf",
          "lem-finite-holonomy-acts-on-a-small-transverse-disk",
          "lem-deck-group-of-the-holonomy-cover-is-the-holonomy-group",
          "def-holonomy-cover-of-a-leaf",
          "prop-quotient-foliation-under-a-free-proper-foliated-action",
          "def-local-transversal-to-a-regular-foliation",
          "prop-products-of-smooth-manifolds-have-a-canonical-product-smooth-structure",
          "def-group-action",
          "def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]]). Let $F$ be a regular\nfoliation, $L$ a compact leaf, $x\\in L$, $T$ a local transversal at $x$ that is\nan embedded disk ([[def-local-transversal-to-a-regular-foliation]]),\n$H=\\operatorname{Hol}(L,x)$ a finite holonomy group,\n$D\\subseteq T$ an $H$-invariant open disk-like neighbourhood as in\n[[lem-finite-holonomy-acts-on-a-small-transverse-disk]], and\n$p:\\widehat L\\to L$ the holonomy cover with\n$\\operatorname{Deck}(p)\\cong H$ acting by the covering-space action of\n[[lem-deck-group-of-the-holonomy-cover-is-the-holonomy-group]]\n([[def-holonomy-cover-of-a-leaf]]).\n\nThe **finite-holonomy normal model** of $(L,T,D,H)$ is the quotient\n$$\\mathcal N:=\\bigl(\\widehat L\\times D\\bigr)\\big/ H,\\qquad h\\cdot(\\hat y,t):=(h\\,\\hat y,\\,h\\,t),$$\nwith the diagonal $H$-action given by the deck action on $\\widehat L$ and the\nholonomy action on $D$, together with the foliation $\\mathcal F_{\\mathcal N}$\nobtained from the product foliation of $\\widehat L\\times D$ by the slices\n$\\widehat L\\times\\{t\\}$, which the diagonal action permutes. The quotient is a\nsmooth foliated manifold: the diagonal action is free and a covering-space\naction and preserves the product foliation, so\n[[prop-quotient-foliation-under-a-free-proper-foliated-action]] applies; the\nproduct carries its canonical product smooth structure\n([[prop-products-of-smooth-manifolds-have-a-canonical-product-smooth-structure]]),\nand the diagonal formula defines an action of the finite group $H$\n([[def-group-action]]).\n\nThe **central leaf** of the model is the image of $\\widehat L\\times\\{x\\}$; it\nis canonically diffeomorphic to $L$, because $\\widehat L/H\\cong L$ by the\ndeck-group lemma and $x$ is fixed by the holonomy action. The leaves of\n$\\mathcal F_{\\mathcal N}$ are the images of the slices $\\widehat L\\times\\{t\\}$;\nthe holonomy group of such a leaf is the finite stabilizer $H_t\\le H$ of $t$,\nand the slice $\\widehat L\\times\\{t\\}$ finitely covers its image because $H_t$\nis finite. Every leaf of the model other than the central one is therefore\nfinitely covered by the holonomy cover of $L$, and all leaves of the model are\ncompact when $L$ is. The model realises $F$ near $L$ in the sense that the\ncentral leaf is $L$ and the local foliation near it is the one induced by the\nproduct foliation of $\\widehat L\\times D$; the descent to $F$ itself is proved\nin the normal-model map lemmas below.",
        "strategy": "Apply the quotient-foliation proposition to the finite group $H$ acting diagonally on $\\widehat L\\times D$: the deck action is a covering-space action and $D$ is $H$-invariant, so the diagonal action is a covering-space action (a product of a covering-space action on $\\widehat L$ and a finite action on $D$) and preserves the product foliation by the slices $\\widehat L\\times\\{t\\}$ because $h\\cdot(\\widehat L\\times\\{t\\})=\\widehat L\\times\\{ht\\}$. The quotient therefore carries a smooth structure and an induced regular foliation of the same codimension. The central leaf is $(\\widehat L\\times\\{x\\})/H\\cong\\widehat L/H\\cong L$ by the deck-group lemma. Holonomy of the model's leaves is computed from the stabilizer $H_t$ in the finite action on $D$, hence is finite; the covering $\\widehat L\\times\\{t\\}\\to(\\widehat L\\times\\{t\\})/H_t$ is finite since $H_t$ is finite. Only the standing $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is used.\n\nHere $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ denotes the countable-choice principle [[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]].",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Ieke Moerdijk and Janez Mr\u010dun, Introduction to Foliations and Lie Groupoids (Cambridge Studies in Advanced Mathematics 91, 2003)",
              "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/introduction-to-foliations-and-lie-groupoids/75BBFED277FF39A56594731202789016",
              "locator": "Design locators: \u00a72.3, pp. 30\u201333 (local Reeb stability and the normal form)"
            },
            {
              "title": "Leiden NCG seminar, Noncommutative Geometry of Foliations (2023 seminar notes; complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://ncg-leiden.github.io/foliation2023/foliation_notes.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a72.1\u2013\u00a72.2, printed pp. 11\u201315 (the normal model as a quotient by finite holonomy)"
            },
            {
              "title": "Rui Loja Fernandes, Compact foliations (notes, PDF)",
              "url": "https://publish.illinois.edu/ruiloja/files/2023/07/compactfoliations.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a71, PDF pp. 1\u20132 (linearization form of Reeb stability); \u00a72, PDF p. 3"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 8,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-transverse-holonomy-transport-is-well-defined-and-equivariant",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Transverse holonomy transport is well defined and equivariant on the model",
        "deps": [
          "lem-a-leafwise-path-determines-a-germ-of-transverse-diffeomorphism",
          "lem-holonomy-germ-is-independent-of-the-foliation-chart-chain",
          "thm-holonomy-depends-only-on-leafwise-homotopy-relative-endpoints",
          "def-holonomy-cover-of-a-leaf",
          "lem-deck-group-of-the-holonomy-cover-is-the-holonomy-group",
          "lem-finite-holonomy-acts-on-a-small-transverse-disk",
          "def-local-transversal-to-a-regular-foliation",
          "def-flat-chart-for-a-distribution",
          "def-plaque-of-a-flat-chart",
          "def-regular-foliation-atlas",
          "thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-maximal-connected-integral-manifolds",
          "def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]]). Let $F$ be a regular\nfoliation, $L$ a compact leaf, $x\\in L$, $T$ a local transversal at $x$ that is\nan embedded disk, $D\\subseteq T$ an $H$-invariant open neighbourhood, and\n$p:\\widehat L\\to L$ the holonomy cover. Then there is a well-defined smooth map\n$$\\Phi:\\widehat L\\times D\\longrightarrow M,\\qquad \\Phi(\\hat y,t)=\\text{endpoint of the leafwise transport of }t\\text{ along a path representing }\\hat y,$$\nwith the following properties: (i) $\\Phi(\\hat y,t)$ lies in the leaf through\nthe initial transverse point $t$ and is obtained by a transverse transport map\ndefined on $D$ along a leafwise path from $x$ to $p(\\hat y)$; (ii) $\\Phi$ is\nwell defined, independently of the choice of path representing $\\hat y$ and of\nthe auxiliary foliation chart chain, by chain independence and homotopy\ninvariance of the holonomy germ; (iii) $\\Phi$ is invariant under the diagonal\naction of $H$, $\\Phi(h\\cdot\\hat y,h\\cdot t)=\\Phi(\\hat y,t)$ for every $h\\in H$,\nwhere $H$ acts on $\\widehat L$ by the deck action and on $D$ by the holonomy\naction; consequently $\\Phi$ descends to the model quotient\n$(\\widehat L\\times D)/H$; (iv) $\\Phi|_{\\widehat L\\times\\{x\\}}=p$; (v) $D$ may be\nshrunk so that all finitely many chart transitions involved are defined on $D$,\nuniformly over $\\widehat L$, because $L$ is compact and the germ family is\n$H$-equivariant.",
        "strategy": "Cover the compact leaf $L$ by finitely many foliation charts and choose the invariant transverse neighbourhood $D$ so small that, in each chart of the covering, the plaque transports are defined on $D$; compactness of $L$ (= compactness of the finite cover and of the finitely many transition maps involved) makes this uniform. For $\\hat y$ represented by a leafwise path $a$ from $x$ to $p(\\hat y)$, set $\\Phi(\\hat y,t):=h_a(t)$, the plaque transport of [[lem-a-leafwise-path-determines-a-germ-of-transverse-diffeomorphism]] transported along $a$. Chain independence and homotopy invariance make this independent of the representative of $\\hat y$. Invariance: fix $h\\in H$ and a loop $\\gamma$ at $x$ with $\\rho_x([\\gamma])=h$, and let $\\hat y$ be represented by a path $a$ from $x$ to $p(\\hat y)$; with the left deck action convention $h\\cdot\\hat y:=\\tau_{h^{-1}}(\\hat y)$ of [[lem-deck-group-of-the-holonomy-cover-is-the-holonomy-group]], the point $h\\cdot\\hat y$ is represented by the concatenation of $\\gamma^{-1}$ with $a$, and transport along that concatenation is $h_a\\circ\\rho_x([\\gamma])^{-1}$, so $\\Phi(h\\cdot\\hat y,h\\cdot t)=h_a(h^{-1}ht)=h_a(t)=\\Phi(\\hat y,t)$; the diagonal quotient identification then makes $\\Phi$ descend to the model. Only the standing $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is used.\n\nHere $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ denotes the countable-choice principle [[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]].",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "literature-derived"
        },
        "sources": {
