      {
        "id": "thm-local-reeb-stability",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "Local Reeb stability for compact leaves with finite holonomy",
        "deps": [
          "def-saturated-neighbourhood-of-a-leaf",
          "def-stable-leaf-of-a-foliation",
          "def-finite-holonomy-normal-model",
          "lem-the-normal-model-map-restricts-to-a-diffeomorphism-onto-a-saturated-neighbourhood",
          "lem-finite-holonomy-acts-on-a-small-transverse-disk",
          "def-regular-foliation-atlas",
          "def-compact-space",
          "def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]]). Let $F$ be a regular\nfoliation of a smooth manifold $M$ and let $L$ be a compact leaf whose holonomy\ngroup is finite. Then $L$ is stable ([[def-stable-leaf-of-a-foliation]]): for\nevery open neighbourhood $W$ of $L$ there is a saturated neighbourhood\n$U\\subseteq W$ of $L$ and a foliated diffeomorphism of $U$ onto an open\nneighbourhood of the central leaf in the finite-holonomy normal model\n$(\\widehat L\\times D)/H$ of [[def-finite-holonomy-normal-model]], carrying $L$\nto the central leaf. Moreover, after shrinking, the neighbourhood $U$ admits a\nretraction $\\pi:U\\to L$ such that for every leaf $L'\\subseteq U$ the restriction\n$\\pi|_{L'}:L'\\to L$ is a finite covering and $\\pi^{-1}(y)$ is a transverse disk\nfor every $y\\in L$; every leaf of $F|_U$ is compact with finite holonomy group\nand is finitely covered by the holonomy cover $\\widehat L$. The hypothesis\nconsumed is finiteness of the holonomy group, not finiteness of $\\pi_1(L)$; no\norientability of $F$ or $M$ is required.",
        "strategy": "Choose a transversal disk $T$ at a point $x\\in L$, shrink it to an $H$-invariant disk $D$ as in [[lem-finite-holonomy-acts-on-a-small-transverse-disk]], build the finite-holonomy normal model, and form the model map $\\overline\\Phi$; the preceding lemmas show that $\\overline\\Phi$ restricts to a foliated diffeomorphism of a neighbourhood of the central leaf onto a saturated neighbourhood of $L$, inside any prescribed $W$. Compactness of $L$ and finiteness of $H$ are used only to obtain the uniform transverse size and the finite covering description. The retraction is induced by the projection of the model to $D$ composed with the identification of the central leaf with $L$; fibres are the $H$-orbits of the deck action, and leaf intersections are finite coverings by the deck-group lemma. Only the standing $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is used.\n\nHere $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ denotes the countable-choice principle [[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]].",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "literature-derived"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Ieke Moerdijk and Janez Mr\u010dun, Introduction to Foliations and Lie Groupoids (Cambridge Studies in Advanced Mathematics 91, 2003)",
              "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/introduction-to-foliations-and-lie-groupoids/75BBFED277FF39A56594731202789016",
              "locator": "Design locators: \u00a72.3, pp. 30\u201333 (local Reeb stability theorem)"
            },
            {
              "title": "Rui Loja Fernandes, Compact foliations (notes, PDF)",
              "url": "https://publish.illinois.edu/ruiloja/files/2023/07/compactfoliations.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a7\u00a71\u20132, PDF pp. 1\u20133 (tubular and retracting forms of local Reeb stability)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 11,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "cor-trivial-holonomy-gives-a-product-foliated-neighbourhood",
        "kind": "corollary",
        "title": "Trivial holonomy gives a product foliated neighbourhood",
        "deps": [
          "thm-local-reeb-stability",
          "def-finite-holonomy-normal-model",
          "def-holonomy-representation-and-holonomy-group-of-a-leaf",
          "def-saturated-neighbourhood-of-a-leaf",
          "prop-products-of-smooth-manifolds-have-a-canonical-product-smooth-structure",
          "def-diffeomorphism-and-local-diffeomorphism-of-manifolds",
          "def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair",
          "def-holonomy-cover-of-a-leaf"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]]). Let $F$ be a regular\nfoliation and $L$ a compact leaf whose holonomy representation is trivial\n(equivalently, whose holonomy group is the trivial group)\n([[def-holonomy-representation-and-holonomy-group-of-a-leaf]]). Then the\nfinite-holonomy normal model with $H=\\{1\\}$ is $\\widehat L\\times D$ with\n$\\widehat L=L$, and $L$ has arbitrarily small saturated neighbourhoods $U$\nfoliated-diffeomorphic to products $L\\times D$ with the product foliation by\nthe slices $L\\times\\{t\\}$. In particular every leaf of $F|_U$ is compact and\ndiffeomorphic to $L$, and $L$ has a fundamental system of product foliated\nneighbourhoods.",
        "strategy": "When $\\rho_x$ is trivial, $\\ker\\rho_x=\\pi_1(L,x)$, so the holonomy cover of [[def-holonomy-cover-of-a-leaf]] has $\\pi_1(\\widehat L)=\\pi_1(L)$ and is the identity cover: $\\widehat L=L$; the deck group is trivial and the model is the product $L\\times D$ with the product foliation. Local Reeb stability then gives the saturated neighbourhood foliated-diffeomorphically to a neighbourhood of the central leaf $L\\times\\{x\\}$, i.e. to some $L\\times D'$ by shrinking $D$. Product neighbourhoods of the form $L\\times D'$ with $D'\\subseteq D$ form a fundamental system. Only the standing $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is used.\n\nHere $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ denotes the countable-choice principle [[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]].",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "literature-derived"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Ieke Moerdijk and Janez Mr\u010dun, Introduction to Foliations and Lie Groupoids (Cambridge Studies in Advanced Mathematics 91, 2003)",
              "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/introduction-to-foliations-and-lie-groupoids/75BBFED277FF39A56594731202789016",
              "locator": "Design locators: \u00a72.3, pp. 30\u201333 (local Reeb stability; trivial-holonomy case)"
            },
            {
              "title": "Danny Calegari, Foliations and the Geometry of 3-Manifolds (Oxford Mathematical Monographs; complete author-hosted PDF)",
              "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/books/foliations/oupbook.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a74.2, printed pp. 140\u2013143 (a leaf with trivial holonomy has a product neighbourhood)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 12,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "cor-finite-fundamental-group-is-a-sufficient-not-necessary-reeb-stability-hypothesis",
        "kind": "corollary",
        "title": "Finiteness of the fundamental group is sufficient, but not necessary, for Reeb stability",
        "deps": [
          "thm-local-reeb-stability",
          "cor-trivial-holonomy-gives-a-product-foliated-neighbourhood",
          "def-holonomy-representation-and-holonomy-group-of-a-leaf",
          "def-based-loops-and-fundamental-group",
          "def-induced-homomorphism-on-fundamental-groups",
          "def-simply-connected",
          "def-stable-leaf-of-a-foliation",
          "def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair",
          "lem-images-of-finitely-generated-and-finite-groups-are-finitely-generated-and-finite"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair, lem-images-of-finitely-generated-and-finite-groups-are-finitely-generated-and-finite]]). Let $F$ be a regular\nfoliation and $L$ a compact leaf. If $\\pi_1(L,x)$ is finite then the holonomy\ngroup $\\operatorname{Hol}(L,x)=\\rho_x(\\pi_1(L,x))$, being a homomorphic image\nof a finite group, is finite; hence $L$ is stable by\n[[thm-local-reeb-stability]]. Finiteness of $\\pi_1(L)$ is therefore sufficient\nfor stability. It is not necessary: the product foliation of $L\\times\\mathbb R^q$\nby the slices $L\\times\\{t\\}$ has compact leaves with trivial holonomy for every\ncompact leaf $L$, including leaves with infinite fundamental group. In\nparticular, for a compact leaf the hypothesis \"finite holonomy\" is strictly\nweaker than \"finite fundamental group\".",
        "strategy": "The image of a finite group under the homomorphism $\\rho_x$ is finite, reducing the sufficient direction to local Reeb stability. For the non-necessity, compute the holonomy of a leaf of a product foliation: transversals are the fibres $\\{y\\}\\times\\mathbb R^q$, the holonomy of any leafwise loop is trivial because the leafwise transport is the identity of the transversal in product coordinates. The product foliation is the trivial case of the construction, and the class of examples with infinite $\\pi_1(L)$ is realised by the companion counterexample. No choice beyond the standing $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is used.\n\nHere $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ denotes the countable-choice principle [[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]].",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "literature-derived"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Ieke Moerdijk and Janez Mr\u010dun, Introduction to Foliations and Lie Groupoids (Cambridge Studies in Advanced Mathematics 91, 2003)",
              "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/introduction-to-foliations-and-lie-groupoids/75BBFED277FF39A56594731202789016",
              "locator": "Design locators: \u00a72.3, pp. 30\u201333 (local Reeb stability; finite \u03c0\u2081 implies finite holonomy)"
            },
            {
              "title": "Leiden NCG seminar, Noncommutative Geometry of Foliations (2023 seminar notes; complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://ncg-leiden.github.io/foliation2023/foliation_notes.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a72.1\u2013\u00a72.2, printed pp. 11\u201315 (product foliations have trivial holonomy)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 13,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-a-compact-codimension-one-leaf-with-finite-fundamental-group-has-trivial-holonomy-when-transversely-oriented",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "In a transversely oriented codimension-one foliation a compact leaf with finite fundamental group has trivial holonomy",
        "deps": [
          "lem-germs-of-orientation-preserving-diffeomorphisms-of-the-line-at-zero-are-torsion-free",
          "def-holonomy-representation-and-holonomy-group-of-a-leaf",
          "def-transversely-oriented-codimension-one-foliation",
          "def-local-transversal-to-a-regular-foliation",
          "def-based-loops-and-fundamental-group",
          "def-induced-homomorphism-on-fundamental-groups",
          "def-compact-space",
          "def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair",
          "cor-trivial-holonomy-gives-a-product-foliated-neighbourhood",
          "lem-images-of-finitely-generated-and-finite-groups-are-finitely-generated-and-finite"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]]). Let $F$ be a\ntransversely oriented codimension-one foliation of a smooth manifold $M$\n([[def-transversely-oriented-codimension-one-foliation]]) and let $L$ be a\ncompact leaf with finite fundamental group\n([[def-based-loops-and-fundamental-group]]). Then the holonomy group of $L$ is\ntrivial, and consequently $L$ has a fundamental system of product foliated\nneighbourhoods $L\\times D$ and every leaf in such a neighbourhood is compact\nand diffeomorphic to $L$.",
        "strategy": "A local transversal $T$ at $x\\in L$ is one-dimensional, and a co-orientation of $F$ orients $T$; the holonomy representation therefore lands in the group of germs of orientation-preserving local diffeomorphisms of $(\\mathbb R,0)$, $\\operatorname{Diff}^+_0(\\mathbb R,0)$. Since $\\pi_1(L,x)$ is finite, its image $H$ is finite; by [[lem-germs-of-orientation-preserving-diffeomorphisms-of-the-line-at-zero-are-torsion-free]] every finite subgroup of $\\operatorname{Diff}^+_0(\\mathbb R,0)$ is trivial, so $H=\\{1\\}$. Triviality of holonomy then gives product foliated neighbourhoods by [[cor-trivial-holonomy-gives-a-product-foliated-neighbourhood]]. Only the standing $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is used.\n\nHere $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ denotes the countable-choice principle [[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]].",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "literature-derived"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Ieke Moerdijk and Janez Mr\u010dun, Introduction to Foliations and Lie Groupoids (Cambridge Studies in Advanced Mathematics 91, 2003)",
              "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/introduction-to-foliations-and-lie-groupoids/75BBFED277FF39A56594731202789016",
              "locator": "Design locators: \u00a72.3, pp. 30\u201333 (local Reeb stability; finite \u03c0\u2081 and co-orientation force trivial holonomy)"
            },
            {
              "title": "Danny Calegari, Foliations and the Geometry of 3-Manifolds (Oxford Mathematical Monographs; complete author-hosted PDF)",
              "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/books/foliations/oupbook.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a74.2, printed pp. 140\u2013143 (one-dimensional transverse germs and torsion-freeness)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 13,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-a-compact-holonomy-free-codimension-one-foliation-is-a-fiber-bundle-over-its-leaf-space",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "A compact holonomy-free codimension-one foliation is fibered over its leaf space",
        "deps": [
          "lem-a-compact-connected-one-dimensional-manifold-without-boundary-is-a-circle",
          "cor-trivial-holonomy-gives-a-product-foliated-neighbourhood",
          "thm-local-reeb-stability",
          "lem-a-compact-codimension-one-leaf-with-finite-fundamental-group-has-trivial-holonomy-when-transversely-oriented",
          "def-saturated-neighbourhood-of-a-leaf",
          "def-quotient-topology",
          "def-connected-space",
          "def-compact-space",
          "def-hausdorff-space",
          "def-smooth-manifold",
          "def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair",
          "thm-smooth-partitions-of-unity-exist-on-manifolds",
          "cor-every-smooth-vector-field-on-a-compact-manifold-is-complete",
          "thm-smooth-dependence-of-ode-solutions-on-parameters"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]]). Let $F$ be a\ncodimension-one foliation of a nonempty closed connected smooth manifold $M$ and\nsuppose that every leaf of $F$ is compact with trivial holonomy and that there\nis a closed smooth manifold $L$ with every leaf diffeomorphic to $L$. Then the\nleaf space $X:=M/F$ with the quotient topology\n([[def-quotient-topology]]) is a compact connected Hausdorff topological\none-manifold, hence homeomorphic to $S^1$\n([[lem-a-compact-connected-one-dimensional-manifold-without-boundary-is-a-circle]]);\nand the quotient map $q:M\\to X$ is a locally trivial fibre bundle with fibre\n$L$: every leaf $L_0=q^{-1}(p)$ has a saturated product neighbourhood\n$U\\cong L_0\\times D$ with $q(U)=:V$ a coordinate interval and $q|_U$ the\nprojection $L_0\\times V\\to V$. Equivalently, $M$ is the total space of a\nlocally trivial fibre bundle over the circle whose fibres are the leaves of\n$F$. Choosing a smooth transverse connection identifies the monodromy with the return diffeomorphism of the entire fibre $L_0$ after one circuit of $X$; its isotopy class is independent of that choice. The foliation is the fibre foliation of that bundle.",
        "strategy": "## Facts & Assumptions\n\n**Given:** A codimension-one foliation $F$ of a closed connected smooth manifold $M$ all of whose leaves are compact with trivial holonomy and diffeomorphic to a fixed closed smooth manifold $L$.\n\n[F1] A compact leaf with trivial (in particular finite) holonomy has a fundamental system of saturated product neighbourhoods $L_0\\times D$, with $D$ an open interval, and every leaf in such a neighbourhood is compact and diffeomorphic to $L_0$ ([[cor-trivial-holonomy-gives-a-product-foliated-neighbourhood]], [[def-saturated-neighbourhood-of-a-leaf]]).\n\n[F2] The leaf space $X=M/F$ is by definition the quotient of $M$ by the equivalence relation \"same leaf\", with the quotient topology; it is compact and connected when $M$ is, and it is Hausdorff when distinct leaves can be separated by saturated open sets ([[def-quotient-topology]], [[def-compact-space]], [[def-connected-space]], [[def-hausdorff-space]], [[def-smooth-manifold]]).\n\n[F3] A nonempty compact connected topological one-manifold without boundary is homeomorphic to $S^1$ ([[lem-a-compact-connected-one-dimensional-manifold-without-boundary-is-a-circle]]).\n\n[F4] The product neighborhoods can be taken smooth, with smooth transverse coordinate changes ([[cor-trivial-holonomy-gives-a-product-foliated-neighbourhood]]).\n\n[F5] Smooth partitions of unity patch local lifts, compact smooth vector fields are complete, and their local ODE flows depend smoothly on parameters ([[thm-smooth-partitions-of-unity-exist-on-manifolds]], [[cor-every-smooth-vector-field-on-a-compact-manifold-is-complete]], [[thm-smooth-dependence-of-ode-solutions-on-parameters]]).\n\n## Proof\n\n**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 (Leaf-space charts and local trivializations.) Let $L_0=q^{-1}(p)$ be a leaf. By [F1] it has a saturated product neighbourhood $U\\cong L_0\\times D$ with $D$ an open interval, and $U$ is a union of leaves, so $q(U)$ is an open subset of $X$ homeomorphic to $D$: the map $q|_U$ is the projection $L_0\\times D\\to D$ followed by the identification $q(U)\\cong D$. These charts make $X$ locally Euclidean of dimension one, and the transition maps between two such charts are the transverse coordinate changes of the foliation, hence homeomorphisms. [F1, F2]\n\n1.2 (Hausdorffness.) Let $p_1\\neq p_2$ in $X$ correspond to distinct leaves $L_1\\neq L_2$. These are disjoint compact subsets of the Hausdorff manifold $M$; choosing saturated product neighbourhoods as in [F1] inside disjoint open neighbourhoods of $L_1$ and $L_2$ gives disjoint open sets $q(U_1)\\ni p_1$ and $q(U_2)\\ni p_2$ in $X$, because a leaf meeting $U_i$ is contained in $U_i$. Hence $X$ is Hausdorff. [F1, F2]\n\n2.1 (Compactness, connectedness, no boundary.) $X$ is compact and connected as a continuous image of $M$ [F2], and by step 1.1 every point of $X$ has a neighbourhood homeomorphic to an open interval, so $X$ has no boundary; by [F3] $X$ is homeomorphic to $S^1$. [F2, F3, step 1.1]\n\n3.1 (Whole-fibre return.) The maps in step 1.1 are local trivializations with fibre $L_0\\cong L$. By F4 their interval coordinate changes are smooth, so $X$ is a smooth circle. Choose a positive base vector field of period one, lift it in the finitely many product trivializations, and patch the lifts with a finite smooth partition of unity. The patched field still projects to the base field. Compactness of $M$ gives its flow for time one, and that flow restricts to a diffeomorphism of the whole fibre $L_0$ onto itself. Flow over $[0,1]$ trivializes the pullback bundle; the endpoint gluing is exactly this return map. Convex interpolation of two such lifts, followed by smooth flow dependence, proves that their return maps are isotopic. A closed transversal is a single curve and does not itself determine a whole-fibre return map. [F1, F4, F5, step 2.1, construct]\n\n4.1 Therefore the leaf space is a compact connected Hausdorff one-manifold homeomorphic to $S^1$ and $q:M\\to X$ is a locally trivial fibre bundle with fibre $L$ whose monodromy is the whole-fibre return map for a chosen transverse connection, with the foliation as its fibre foliation. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 2.1, step 3.1] \u220e\n",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "literature-derived"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Danny Calegari, Foliations and the Geometry of 3-Manifolds (Oxford Mathematical Monographs; complete author-hosted PDF)",
              "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/books/foliations/oupbook.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a74.2, printed pp. 140\u2013143 (the leaf space of a foliation with all leaves compact and trivial holonomy)"
            },
            {
              "title": "Ieke Moerdijk and Janez Mr\u010dun, Introduction to Foliations and Lie Groupoids (Cambridge Studies in Advanced Mathematics 91, 2003)",
              "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/introduction-to-foliations-and-lie-groupoids/75BBFED277FF39A56594731202789016",
              "locator": "Design locators: \u00a72.5, pp. 44\u201351 (global Reeb stability and the fibration over the circle)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 14,
        "justified_by": []
