        "dependency_level": 3,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "ex-finite-holonomy-mobius-normal-model",
        "kind": "example",
        "title": "The finite-holonomy normal model of the M\u00f6bius band",
        "deps": [
          "def-finite-holonomy-normal-model",
          "lem-finite-holonomy-acts-on-a-small-transverse-disk",
          "lem-deck-group-of-the-holonomy-cover-is-the-holonomy-group",
          "prop-quotient-foliation-under-a-free-proper-foliated-action",
          "def-holonomy-representation-and-holonomy-group-of-a-leaf",
          "def-holonomy-cover-of-a-leaf",
          "def-local-transversal-to-a-regular-foliation",
          "def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]]). Let\n$$M:=\\bigl(S^1\\times(-1,1)\\bigr)/\\mathbb Z_2$$\nbe the M\u00f6bius band, the quotient by the free involution\n$\\tau(u,v):=(u+\\tfrac12,-v)$, foliated by the images of the circles\n$S^1\\times\\{v\\}$. The central leaf $L_0$ (image of $v=0$) is the circle\n$\\mathbb R/(\\tfrac12\\mathbb Z)$ whose holonomy group is $\\mathbb Z_2$,\ngenerated by the reflection germ $v\\mapsto-v$ of a transversal interval: going\nonce around the central leaf identifies the transversal coordinate $v$ with\n$-v$ ([[def-holonomy-representation-and-holonomy-group-of-a-leaf]]). Its\nholonomy cover is the connected double cover\n$\\widehat L_0=S^1\\to L_0$\n([[def-holonomy-cover-of-a-leaf]]), the deck group is $\\mathbb Z_2$ acting by\n$u\\mapsto u+\\tfrac12$, and the finite-holonomy normal model\n$(\\widehat L_0\\times D)/\\mathbb Z_2$ with the diagonal action\n$(u,v)\\mapsto(u+\\tfrac12,-v)$ is exactly the M\u00f6bius band with its foliation:\nthe quotient recovers the surface and the central leaf\n$(\\widehat L_0\\times\\{0\\})/\\mathbb Z_2\\cong L_0$. The other leaves are images\nof $S^1\\times\\{v\\}$ and double-cover the central leaf, as predicted by the\nfinite-holonomy model.",
        "strategy": "Verification: (1) the involution $(u,v)\\mapsto(u+\\tfrac12,-v)$ is free on $S^1\\times(-1,1)$ (it has no fixed point: $v=-v$ would force $v=0$, and then $u=u+\\tfrac12$ is impossible on $S^1$), so the quotient is a smooth surface, in fact the M\u00f6bius band, the total space of the unique non-trivial interval bundle over $S^1$; (2) the foliation of $S^1\\times(-1,1)$ by the slices $S^1\\times\\{v\\}$ is mapped to itself by the involution (up to the reflection $v\\mapsto-v$), and [[prop-quotient-foliation-under-a-free-proper-foliated-action]] descends it; (3) the central leaf is $\\mathbb R/(\\tfrac12\\mathbb Z)\\cong S^1$, and following it once around (from $u$ to $u+\\tfrac12$) returns the transversal coordinate $v$ to $-v$, so the return germ is the reflection $v\\mapsto-v$ and the holonomy group is $\\mathbb Z_2$; (4) the holonomy cover has deck group $\\mathbb Z_2$ acting by $u\\mapsto u+\\tfrac12$ by [[lem-deck-group-of-the-holonomy-cover-is-the-holonomy-group]], so the normal model is the diagonal quotient $(\\widehat L_0\\times D)/\\mathbb Z_2$, which is the M\u00f6bius band by the very definition of the quotient; (5) leaves other than the central one are the images of the slices $v\\neq0$ and meet a small transversal in two points, i.e. double-cover $L_0$. The example therefore tests the normal model and the deck-group description. No choice beyond the standing $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is used.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "literature-derived"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Leiden NCG seminar, Noncommutative Geometry of Foliations (2023 seminar notes; complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://ncg-leiden.github.io/foliation2023/foliation_notes.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a72.1\u2013\u00a72.2, printed pp. 11\u201315 (the M\u00f6bius band as a quotient foliation with finite holonomy)"
            },
            {
              "title": "Ieke Moerdijk and Janez Mr\u010dun, Introduction to Foliations and Lie Groupoids (Cambridge Studies in Advanced Mathematics 91, 2003)",
              "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/introduction-to-foliations-and-lie-groupoids/75BBFED277FF39A56594731202789016",
              "locator": "Design locators: \u00a72.3, pp. 30\u201333 (finite-holonomy model)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 9,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "cex-a-reeb-component-has-a-compact-boundary-leaf-with-infinite-holonomy-behaviour",
        "kind": "counterexample",
        "title": "A Reeb component has a compact boundary leaf with infinite holonomy",
        "deps": [
          "prop-reeb-foliation-of-the-solid-torus-has-the-boundary-as-a-leaf",
          "def-stable-leaf-of-a-foliation",
          "def-saturated-neighbourhood-of-a-leaf",
          "def-holonomy-representation-and-holonomy-group-of-a-leaf",
          "def-two-dimensional-torus",
          "def-local-transversal-to-a-regular-foliation",
          "thm-local-reeb-stability",
          "def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. The Reeb foliation of the solid torus $X=\\overline D^2\\times S^1$ ([[prop-reeb-foliation-of-the-solid-torus-has-the-boundary-as-a-leaf]]) has a compact leaf, the boundary torus $\\partial X\\cong T^2$, whose holonomy group is infinite: the holonomy of the loop in the $S^1$-factor is represented by the non-identity contraction germ determined by $u(r')=u(r)+1$, $u(r)=\\exp(1/(1-r^2))$. Hence the compact leaf does not have finite holonomy, the hypothesis of [[thm-local-reeb-stability]] cannot be dropped, and the leaf is not stable: near the boundary leaf the interior leaves are planes that accumulate on it, so no saturated neighbourhood of the boundary leaf consists of compact leaves. The example also shows that the failure is not a failure of compactness of the ambient foliated manifold but a failure of finiteness of holonomy.",
        "strategy": "Counterexample verification: (1) the boundary torus is a leaf by construction; (2) the holonomy of its $S^1$-direction loop is computed in the cited proposition and is a non-identity contraction, so the holonomy group is infinite; (3) if the boundary leaf were stable, a saturated neighbourhood of it would consist of leaves diffeomorphic to it (compact), but every interior leaf is a plane accumulating on the boundary, contradiction; (4) therefore local Reeb stability genuinely needs finite holonomy, and the Reeb component is the standard boundary case. No choice beyond the standing $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is used.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "literature-derived"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Leiden NCG seminar, Noncommutative Geometry of Foliations (2023 seminar notes; complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://ncg-leiden.github.io/foliation2023/foliation_notes.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a72.1\u2013\u00a72.2, printed pp. 11\u201315 (the Reeb component: compact boundary leaf with infinite holonomy)"
            },
            {
              "title": "Danny Calegari, Foliations and the Geometry of 3-Manifolds (Oxford Mathematical Monographs; complete author-hosted PDF)",
              "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/books/foliations/oupbook.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a74.3, Example 4.7, printed pp. 144\u2013145 (the Reeb component)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 12,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "ex-product-foliation-near-a-compact-trivial-holonomy-leaf",
        "kind": "example",
        "title": "The product foliation near a compact leaf with trivial holonomy",
        "deps": [
          "cor-trivial-holonomy-gives-a-product-foliated-neighbourhood",
          "thm-local-reeb-stability",
          "def-regular-foliation-atlas",
          "def-two-dimensional-torus",
          "def-euclidean-spheres-and-closed-balls",
          "prop-products-of-smooth-manifolds-have-a-canonical-product-smooth-structure",
          "def-local-transversal-to-a-regular-foliation",
          "cor-fundamental-group-of-two-dimensional-torus",
          "def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$\n([[def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair]]). Let $L=S^1$ and\nconsider the product foliation of $M=S^1\\times\\mathbb R$ by the circles\n$L_t=S^1\\times\\{t\\}$. Each leaf is compact and has trivial holonomy: a local\ntransversal is a vertical interval $\\{u\\}\\times(-\\varepsilon,\\varepsilon)$ and\nthe holonomy of any leafwise loop is the identity. For every leaf $L_{t_0}$ and\nevery $\\delta>0$ the open set\n$U_\\delta=S^1\\times(t_0-\\delta,t_0+\\delta)$ is a saturated neighbourhood of\n$L_{t_0}$ foliated-diffeomorphic to the product $S^1\\times(-\\delta,\\delta)$\nwith the product foliation, so the conclusion of\n[[cor-trivial-holonomy-gives-a-product-foliated-neighbourhood]] is realised\nexactly. The same computation with $L=T^2$ gives compact leaves with infinite\nfundamental group and trivial holonomy, showing that trivial holonomy does not\nrequire finiteness of $\\pi_1$.",
        "strategy": "Verification: (1) the slices $S^1\\times\\{t\\}$ are the maximal connected integral manifolds of the kernel of $dt$, so they are the leaves; (2) a leafwise loop lies in a slice and the plaque transport in product coordinates is the identity, so the holonomy representation is trivial; (3) $U_\\delta$ is open, saturated (a union of slices) and is identified with the product foliation by the identity in product coordinates; (4) for $L=T^2$ the same argument applies and [[cor-fundamental-group-of-two-dimensional-torus]] gives infinite $\\pi_1$ of the leaf. The example therefore tests the product corollary and the distinction between finite holonomy and finite fundamental group. No choice principle beyond the standing $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is used.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "literature-derived"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Leiden NCG seminar, Noncommutative Geometry of Foliations (2023 seminar notes; complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://ncg-leiden.github.io/foliation2023/foliation_notes.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a72.1\u2013\u00a72.2, printed pp. 11\u201315 (product foliations have trivial holonomy)"
            },
            {
              "title": "Danny Calegari, Foliations and the Geometry of 3-Manifolds (Oxford Mathematical Monographs; complete author-hosted PDF)",
              "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/books/foliations/oupbook.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a74.2, printed pp. 140\u2013143 (transverse transport in product coordinates)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 13,
        "justified_by": []
      },
      {
        "id": "cex-a-compact-leaf-with-infinite-fundamental-group-can-still-have-trivial-holonomy",
        "kind": "counterexample",
        "title": "A compact leaf with infinite fundamental group can still have trivial holonomy",
        "deps": [
          "cor-finite-fundamental-group-is-a-sufficient-not-necessary-reeb-stability-hypothesis",
          "cor-trivial-holonomy-gives-a-product-foliated-neighbourhood",
          "def-two-dimensional-torus",
          "cor-fundamental-group-of-two-dimensional-torus",
          "prop-products-of-smooth-manifolds-have-a-canonical-product-smooth-structure",
          "def-holonomy-representation-and-holonomy-group-of-a-leaf",
          "def-based-loops-and-fundamental-group",
          "def-countable-choice-principle-for-foliation-pair"
        ],
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $M=T^2\\times S^1$ with the product foliation by the tori $L_\\theta=T^2\\times\\{\\theta\\}$. Every leaf $L_\\theta$ is compact with $\\pi_1(L_\\theta)\\cong\\mathbb Z^2$ infinite ([[cor-fundamental-group-of-two-dimensional-torus]]), yet its holonomy group is trivial: a transversal is a vertical circle segment $\\{p\\}\\times(-\\varepsilon,\\varepsilon)$ and the leafwise transport in product coordinates is the identity. Hence the leaf is stable with a product foliated neighbourhood $T^2\\times(-\\varepsilon,\\varepsilon)$ ([[cor-trivial-holonomy-gives-a-product-foliated-neighbourhood]]), and finiteness of the fundamental group is not necessary for stability. Together with the Reeb component counterexample this pins the exact hypothesis of local Reeb stability to finiteness of the holonomy group.",
        "strategy": "Counterexample verification: (1) the slices are the leaves of the product foliation; (2) $\\pi_1(T^2)\\cong\\mathbb Z^2$ is infinite; (3) holonomy around any leafwise loop is the identity of the transversal because the foliation is a product; (4) the product neighbourhood is saturated and foliated as a product, so the leaf is stable. The example therefore separates 'finite fundamental group' from 'stable/trivial holonomy'. No choice beyond the standing $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ is used.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "literature-derived"
        },
        "sources": {
          "scraped": [],
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Leiden NCG seminar, Noncommutative Geometry of Foliations (2023 seminar notes; complete PDF)",
              "url": "https://ncg-leiden.github.io/foliation2023/foliation_notes.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a72.1\u2013\u00a72.2, printed pp. 11\u201315 (product foliations and trivial holonomy)"
            },
            {
              "title": "Danny Calegari, Foliations and the Geometry of 3-Manifolds (Oxford Mathematical Monographs; complete author-hosted PDF)",
              "url": "https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/books/foliations/oupbook.pdf",
              "locator": "\u00a74.2, printed pp. 140\u2013143 (trivial transverse transport in product foliations)"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 14,
        "justified_by": []
      }
    ]
  }
